var e=JSON.parse(`[[2601,"The main RxJS operators include map for transforming data, filter for filtering, switchMap for switching to a new stream, mergeMap for parallel processing, catchError for handling errors and debounceTime for a delay. They are applied in a chain through pipe, for managing asynchronous events.","The main RxJS operators and their application\\nRxJS provides powerful operators for working with asynchronous streams of data (Observables). They let you transform, filter and combine streams and handle errors in them, which is especially useful in Angular and React for managing events, HTTP requests and states.\\n\\nThe popular operators\\n\\n- map — it transforms every value of the stream; it extracts a field from an object, for example.\\n- filter — it passes only the values that satisfy a condition.\\n- switchMap — it cancels the previous inner Observable when a new value arrives; it is ideal for a search.\\n- mergeMap — it processes all of the inner Observables in parallel; it suits independent requests.\\n- catchError — it intercepts errors and returns a fallback Observable.\\n- debounceTime — it delays the emit until a pause has passed; it is useful for inputs.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nRxJS's operators are indispensable for reactive programming, making the management of complex asynchronous scenarios easier — autocompletion, the handling of clicks or sequential requests. Their use improves the readability and the reliability of the code.","import { fromEvent, of } from 'rxjs';\\nimport { map, filter, debounceTime, switchMap, catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';\\n\\nconst input = document.getElementById('search');\\nconst search$ = fromEvent(input, 'input').pipe(\\n  map(event => event.target.value),\\n  filter(value => value.length > 2),\\n  debounceTime(300),\\n  switchMap(query => fetch(\`/api/search?q=\${query}\`).then(res => res.json())),\\n  catchError(err => of({ error: err.message }))\\n);\\n\\nsearch$.subscribe(data => console.log(data));"],[2602,"There are two main approaches: server-side and client-side processing of the data. With server-side pagination the backend returns only the current page, while the frontend sends the filtering and sorting parameters. Client-side pagination suits small sets of data, when all of the data is loaded at once and processed on the browser's side. The choice depends on the volume of the data and on the performance requirements.","The main approaches to working with tables\\nWhen developing frontend applications, work with tabular data requires the choice of a strategy for handling the filtering, the sorting and the pagination. There are two main approaches: server-side and client-side. Each of them has its own advantages and limitations.\\n\\nServer-side processing of the data\\nWith the server-side approach all of the operations are performed on the backend. The frontend sends HTTP requests with the parameters: the page number, the page size, the fields for the sorting, the filters. The backend returns only the portion of the data requested plus the overall number of records.\\n\\nClient-side processing of the data\\nWith the client-side approach all of the data is loaded in one request, and the filtering, the sorting and the pagination are performed in the browser. That suits tables with a small volume of data (up to a few thousand records).\\n\\nThe hybrid approach\\nA combination is often used: the initial load with server-side pagination, and then client-side filtering of the data already loaded. That lets you cut the number of requests down while preserving the performance.\\n\\nThe choice of approach depends on the volume of the data, the frequency of the updates and the requirements on the UX. For large tables (10,000+ records) server-side pagination is preferable. For small reference lists or configurations, client-side processing is enough.","// An example of a request to an API with parameters\\nconst fetchUsers = async (page, pageSize, filters) => {\\n  const params = new URLSearchParams({\\n    page,\\n    pageSize,\\n    sortBy: 'name',\\n    sortOrder: 'asc',\\n    search: filters.search || ''\\n  });\\n  const response = await fetch(\`/api/users?\${params}\`);\\n  const data = await response.json();\\n  return {\\n    items: data.users,\\n    total: data.totalCount\\n  };\\n};\\n\\n// An example of client-side filtering and pagination\\nconst filterAndPaginate = (data, page, pageSize, searchTerm) => {\\n  const filtered = data.filter(item =>\\n    item.name.toLowerCase().includes(searchTerm.toLowerCase())\\n  );\\n  const start = (page - 1) * pageSize;\\n  return {\\n    items: filtered.slice(start, start + pageSize),\\n    total: filtered.length\\n  };\\n};"],[2603,"Redux Observable is a middleware for Redux, which uses RxJS for handling asynchronous actions and side effects. It lets you create \\"epics\\" — functions that take a stream of actions and return a new stream of actions. That makes work with complex asynchronous scenarios easier, such as the cancellation of requests or a debounce.","What is Redux Observable?\\nRedux Observable is a middleware for Redux, which integrates the RxJS library for managing side effects. Rather than writing complex logic in the action creators or the reducers, you define \\"epics\\" — functions that take a stream of all of the actions (action$) and return a new stream of actions. That lets you handle asynchronous operations such as HTTP requests, timers or WebSockets, using RxJS's powerful operators.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nEpics are pure functions, which receive a stream of actions and return a stream of actions. They do not change the state directly but only dispatch new actions. You can create an epic that listens for the FETCH_USER action, makes a request to an API and dispatches FETCH_USER_SUCCESS or FETCH_USER_FAILURE, for example.\\n\\nWhy use Redux Observable?\\nRedux Observable is especially useful when you have to manage complex asynchronous flows, such as the cancellation of requests, a debounce, a throttle or the combination of several sources of data. RxJS provides a rich set of operators for working with time and events, which makes the code more declarative and easier to test.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\nSuppose you have a search box, which sends requests on every input. Without Redux Observable you would use a debounce by hand, but with RxJS that is done simply:\\n\\nHere debounceTime(300) delays the handling by 300 ms, while switchMap cancels the previous request if a new one has arrived.\\n\\nConclusion\\nRedux Observable is worth applying when your application requires complex asynchronous logic, such as the cancellation of requests, the combination of streams or work in real time. It makes the code more predictable and more testable, but it requires an understanding of RxJS.","import { ofType } from 'redux-observable';\\nimport { ajax } from 'rxjs/ajax';\\nimport { map, switchMap, catchError } from 'rxjs/operators';\\nimport { of } from 'rxjs';\\n\\nconst fetchUserEpic = (action$) =>\\n  action$.pipe(\\n    ofType('FETCH_USER'),\\n    switchMap((action) =>\\n      ajax.getJSON(\`/api/users/\${action.payload}\`).pipe(\\n        map((response) => ({\\n          type: 'FETCH_USER_SUCCESS',\\n          payload: response,\\n        })),\\n        catchError((error) =>\\n          of({\\n            type: 'FETCH_USER_FAILURE',\\n            payload: error.message,\\n          })\\n        )\\n      )\\n    )\\n  );\\n\\nconst searchEpic = (action$) =>\\n  action$.pipe(\\n    ofType('SEARCH'),\\n    debounceTime(300),\\n    switchMap((action) =>\\n      ajax.getJSON(\`/api/search?q=\${action.payload}\`).pipe(\\n        map((response) => ({\\n          type: 'SEARCH_SUCCESS',\\n          payload: response,\\n        }))\\n      )\\n    )\\n  );"],[2604,"A memory leak arises when a global object holds a reference to an event handler that refers to a removed DOM element. To avoid that, you have to remove the handler explicitly through removeEventListener before removing the element. You can also use weak references (WeakRef) or clear the reference in the global object after the element has been removed.","The problem of a memory leak when removing a DOM element\\nWhen you remove a DOM element from the document but a global object (window or a global variable, for example) keeps holding a reference to an event handler that was bound to that element, a memory leak arises. The event handler closes over a reference to the element, and the garbage collector cannot release the memory, since there is still an active reference to the element through the chain: the global object → the handler → the element.\\n\\nHow this happens in practice\\nLet us look at an example: you add a click handler to a button and save it in a global array. After the button is removed from the DOM, the handler stays in the array, holding a reference to the removed element.\\n\\nThe ways of solving it\\n\\n- The explicit removal of the handler: before removing the element, call removeEventListener, passing in the very same handler function.\\n- Clearing the global reference: after the element has been removed, remove the handler from the global object (set it to null or remove it from the array, for example).\\n- The use of WeakRef: in the modern browsers you can hold a weak reference to the handler, which does not hinder the garbage collection.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo prevent memory leaks, always remove the event handlers before removing DOM elements, especially if they are held in global data structures. That is critical for long-lived applications (SPAs) and when working with dynamic interfaces.","const handlers = [];\\nconst button = document.getElementById('myButton');\\nconst handler = () => console.log('click');\\nbutton.addEventListener('click', handler);\\nhandlers.push(handler);\\n// We remove the element\\nbutton.remove();\\n// The handler is still in handlers, so the element is not removed from memory\\n\\n// The solution: we remove the handler before removing the element\\nbutton.removeEventListener('click', handler);\\nbutton.remove();\\n// Or we clear the global reference\\nhandlers = handlers.filter(h => h !== handler);"],[2605,"Yes, for offline mode the requests are cached by means of a Service Worker and the Cache API. The 'Cache First' strategy lets you check the cache first and make a network request when the data is absent. That lets the application work without the internet and speeds the loading up.","The caching of requests for offline mode\\nTo let a web application work without the internet, a combination of a Service Worker and the Cache API is used. A Service Worker is a script that works in the background and intercepts the network requests. It lets you implement various caching strategies, such as 'Cache First' (the cache first, then the network) or 'Network First' (the network first, and the cache on an error).\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\n\\n- Progressive web applications (PWAs) for mobile devices\\n- Applications with an unstable internet connection (maps, news, for example)\\n- Speeding the loading of repeat visits up\\n\\nConclusion: the caching of requests through a Service Worker is necessary for creating reliable offline applications. It improves the user experience and reduces the load on the network, especially in conditions of a bad connection.","// service-worker.js\\nself.addEventListener('install', (event) => {\\n  event.waitUntil(\\n    caches.open('my-cache-v1').then((cache) => {\\n      return cache.addAll([\\n        '/',\\n        '/styles.css',\\n        '/script.js',\\n        '/offline.html'\\n      ]);\\n    })\\n  );\\n});\\n\\nself.addEventListener('fetch', (event) => {\\n  event.respondWith(\\n    caches.match(event.request).then((cachedResponse) => {\\n      // We return the cache if it is there\\n      if (cachedResponse) {\\n        return cachedResponse;\\n      }\\n      // Otherwise we make a network request\\n      return fetch(event.request).catch(() => {\\n        // If the network is unavailable, we show the offline page\\n        return caches.match('/offline.html');\\n      });\\n    })\\n  );\\n});"],[2606,"In React, local state, the Context API and libraries such as Redux, MobX and Zustand are used for managing state. Redux is based on the Flux architecture: a single store, immutable state, pure reducer functions and a unidirectional data flow. Actions are dispatched, the reducers create the new state, and the components subscribe to the changes.","The approaches to state management in React\\nIn React applications the state can be local (useState, useReducer) or global. For global state the Context API is used, which is built into React, or specialized libraries: Redux, MobX, Zustand, Recoil, Jotai. The choice depends on the application's complexity: for simple projects the Context API is enough; for large ones Redux or MobX.\\n\\nRedux's architecture\\nRedux implements the Flux pattern with a single source of truth — the store. The state is available for reading only, and the changes happen through pure reducer functions. The data flow is unidirectional: a component dispatches an action, the action reaches the reducer, the reducer creates the new state, and the store notifies the subscribers.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nRedux suits large applications with a great many interrelated pieces of state, where the predictability and the debugging matter. For small projects it is better to use the Context API or lighter libraries such as Zustand.","// action types\\nconst ADD_TODO = 'ADD_TODO';\\n\\n// action creator\\nconst addTodo = (text) => ({\\n  type: ADD_TODO,\\n  payload: text\\n});\\n\\n// reducer\\nconst todosReducer = (state = [], action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case ADD_TODO:\\n      return [...state, { text: action.payload, completed: false }];\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// store\\nimport { createStore } from 'redux';\\nconst store = createStore(todosReducer);\\n\\n// dispatch\\nstore.dispatch(addTodo('Learn Redux'));"],[2607,"Lifting state up is a pattern in React in which the state shared by several components is moved into their nearest common ancestor. The parent component manages that state and passes it to the child components through props, and also provides the functions for changing it. That lets you synchronize the data between the components without using a global store. The pattern makes the data flow predictable and makes debugging easier.","What is lifting state up?\\nLifting state up is a fundamental pattern in React, which is used when two or more components have to synchronize their data. Rather than holding the state in every component separately, it is moved (lifted) into the nearest common parent. The parent becomes the single source of truth and passes the data and the callbacks for changing the state down through props.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nLet us look at an example: two \`TemperatureInput\` components, for entering the temperature in Celsius and in Fahrenheit. If each held its own state, they would not be able to synchronize. The solution is to lift the state into a \`Calculator\` component:\\n\\nHere \`Calculator\` holds a single piece of state (\`temperature\` and \`scale\`) and passes it into both \`TemperatureInput\`s. When the value in either field changes, the corresponding callback is called, which updates the state in the parent, and both fields repaint in sync.\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nThe pattern is used in any situation in which components at the same level have to exchange data: forms with dependent fields, lists with filtering, toolbars and so on. It is an alternative to global state managers (Redux, Context) for the simple cases.\\n\\nConclusion\\nLifting state up is a simple, effective way of managing shared data in React without external libraries. It makes the data flow unidirectional and predictable, which makes understanding and debugging the application easier. Use it when several components have to be synchronized but there is no need for a global store.","function TemperatureInput({ scale, temperature, onTemperatureChange }) {\\n  return (\\n    <fieldset>\\n      <legend>{scale === 'c' ? 'Celsius' : 'Fahrenheit'}</legend>\\n      <input value={temperature}\\n             onChange={(e) => onTemperatureChange(e.target.value)} />\\n    </fieldset>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Calculator() {\\n  const [temperature, setTemperature] = useState('');\\n  const [scale, setScale] = useState('c');\\n\\n  const handleCelsiusChange = (value) => {\\n    setTemperature(value);\\n    setScale('c');\\n  };\\n\\n  const handleFahrenheitChange = (value) => {\\n    setTemperature(value);\\n    setScale('f');\\n  };\\n\\n  const celsius = scale === 'f' ? tryConvert(temperature, toCelsius) : temperature;\\n  const fahrenheit = scale === 'c' ? tryConvert(temperature, toFahrenheit) : temperature;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <TemperatureInput scale=\\"c\\" temperature={celsius} onTemperatureChange={handleCelsiusChange} />\\n      <TemperatureInput scale=\\"f\\" temperature={fahrenheit} onTemperatureChange={handleFahrenheitChange} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2608,"The validation of multi-step forms requires the management of every step's state plus an overall validation on the submission. The problems include the synchronization of the data between the steps, the handling of going back, and complex validation rules. The testing includes unit tests for every step, integration tests for the flow, and tests for the edge cases.","The validation of multi-step forms is the process of checking the data at every stage of the input, and also on the final submission. The main difficulty is that the data can be incomplete at the intermediate steps while still having to be correct for the move to the next step. That requires a flexible validation system, which takes the context of the current step into account.\\n\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- The synchronization of the state: every step's data has to be consistent with the rest. If a country is chosen at the first step, for example, then at the second step the list of cities has to update.\\n- Going back: the user can return to the previous step and change the data, which can affect the validity of the following steps.\\n- Complex rules: the validation can depend on the data from different steps — a check of the age depending on the role chosen, for example.\\n\\nThe approaches to the implementation\\nOne widespread approach is to use a single state object for the whole form and to validate only the fields of the current step. For managing the state, you can use libraries such as React Hook Form or Formik. An example in React:\\n\\nThe testing\\nFor testing multi-step forms, use:\\n- Unit tests: they check the validation of every step in isolation.\\n- Integration tests: they check the complete flow: the transitions between the steps, going back, the submission of the form.\\n- Tests for the edge cases: empty fields, wrong formats of data, a repeat submission.\\n\\nConclusion: multi-step forms require careful management of the state and the validation. Use specialized libraries in order to make it easier, and carry the testing out at all levels, so as to avoid regressions.","const [formData, setFormData] = useState({});\\nconst [currentStep, setCurrentStep] = useState(0);\\n\\nconst validateStep = (step) => {\\n  const fields = steps[step].fields;\\n  return fields.every(field => formData[field] !== undefined && formData[field] !== '');\\n};\\n\\nconst handleNext = () => {\\n  if (validateStep(currentStep)) {\\n    setCurrentStep(prev => prev + 1);\\n  }\\n};"],[2609,"There are several popular libraries for validating forms. Yup and Zod are used for validation schemas, often with React Hook Form. Joi is a powerful library for Node.js. Formik provides built-in validation. The choice depends on the ecosystem and on the project's requirements.","The libraries for validating forms\\nThe validation of forms is a key part of the user experience, preventing the submission of incorrect data. There are a great many libraries that make that process easier. The most popular include Yup, Zod and Joi, together with the integrations with React Hook Form and Formik.\\n\\nThe main libraries\\n\\n- Yup — a lightweight library for creating validation schemas; it is often used with React Hook Form. It supports chains of methods and custom error messages.\\n- Zod — a modern alternative to Yup with a TypeScript-oriented API, which provides strict typing and better performance.\\n- Joi — a powerful library for Node.js, popular in backend development, with a rich set of validation rules.\\n- React Hook Form — not a validation library in itself, but it integrates with Yup, Zod and others for managing forms and validation.\\n- Formik — a library for managing forms in React; it has built-in validation through Yup or through custom functions.\\n\\nAn example of the code with Yup and React Hook Form\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of library depends on the technology stack: for React projects with TypeScript Zod is preferable; for simple forms Yup; and for a backend in Node.js Joi. These tools speed the development up and improve the application's reliability.","import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';\\nimport { yupResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/yup';\\nimport * as yup from 'yup';\\n\\nconst schema = yup.object().shape({\\n  email: yup.string().email('Invalid email').required('Required'),\\n  password: yup.string().min(6, 'Min 6 chars').required(),\\n});\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const { register, handleSubmit, errors } = useForm({\\n    resolver: yupResolver(schema),\\n  });\\n  const onSubmit = (data) => console.log(data);\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>\\n      <input {...register('email')} />\\n      {errors.email && <p>{errors.email.message}</p>}\\n      <input type=\\"password\\" {...register('password')} />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2610,"This question assesses your experience of displaying data in a user interface. The candidate should talk about the creation of charts, tables and complex forms. It matters to show an understanding of the performance, the accessibility and the usability of such components.","Experience of displaying data in the UI\\nThe display of data in a user interface is a key task for a frontend developer. It includes the creation of charts, tables, complex forms and other visual elements for presenting information. It matters not only to display the data correctly but also to ensure the performance, the accessibility and the usability.\\n\\nThe main aspects\\n\\n- Charts and diagrams: libraries such as Chart.js, D3.js, Recharts or ECharts are used. It matters to be able to configure the axes, the legends and the animations and to handle large volumes of data.\\n- Tables: complex tables with sorting, filtering, pagination and the editing of cells. The popular libraries: React Table, AG Grid, Handsontable.\\n- Complex forms: multi-step forms, dynamic fields, validation, custom controls (a date picker or an autocomplete, for example).\\n\\nAn example of the code: a simple table with sorting in React\\n\\nConclusion\\nExperience of displaying data in the UI is necessary for creating informative, convenient interfaces. Apply specialized libraries for the complex components, and always take the performance into account when working with large volumes of data.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Table = ({ data }) => {\\n  const [sortField, setSortField] = useState(null);\\n  const [sortOrder, setSortOrder] = useState('asc');\\n\\n  const sortedData = [...data].sort((a, b) => {\\n    if (sortField) {\\n      const compare = a[sortField] > b[sortField] ? 1 : -1;\\n      return sortOrder === 'asc' ? compare : -compare;\\n    }\\n    return 0;\\n  });\\n\\n  const handleSort = (field) => {\\n    setSortOrder(sortField === field && sortOrder === 'asc' ? 'desc' : 'asc');\\n    setSortField(field);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <table>\\n      <thead>\\n        <tr>\\n          <th onClick={() => handleSort('name')}>Name</th>\\n          <th onClick={() => handleSort('age')}>Age</th>\\n        </tr>\\n      </thead>\\n      <tbody>\\n        {sortedData.map((row, idx) => (\\n          <tr key={idx}>\\n            <td>{row.name}</td>\\n            <td>{row.age}</td>\\n          </tr>\\n        ))}\\n      </tbody>\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport default Table;"],[2611,"In JavaScript and React the libraries moment.js, date-fns and dayjs are often used for working with dates. Moment.js provides extensive functionality but counts as outdated. date-fns offers modular functions for working with dates. dayjs is a lightweight alternative with a similar API.","The libraries for working with dates in JS/React\\nWork with dates in JavaScript can be difficult, because of the particulars of time zones, formatting and computation. To make those tasks easier, developers use specialized libraries. The most popular of them: moment.js, date-fns and dayjs.\\n\\nMoment.js\\nMoment.js was the standard for working with dates for a long time. It provides a convenient API for parsing, formatting, manipulating and displaying dates. The library counts as outdated, however, because of its large size and its performance problems. Developers recommend moving to more modern alternatives.\\n\\ndate-fns\\ndate-fns is a modular library, which lets you import only the functions you need, which reduces the size of the bundle. It offers pure functions, without the mutation of the original objects. date-fns suits modern projects well, where the performance and tree shaking matter.\\n\\nDay.js\\nDay.js is a lightweight library (about 2 KB), which has an API similar to moment.js's. It supports plugins for extending the functionality, such as relative time or time zones. Day.js is ideal for projects where a minimal bundle size is required.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of library depends on the project's requirements: for large applications with an emphasis on the performance use date-fns; for small projects with simple tasks dayjs; and for supporting old code moment.js. In React these libraries are often applied for formatting dates in components, for working with calendars or for displaying timestamps.","// An example of the usage of moment.js\\nconst moment = require('moment');\\nconst now = moment();\\nconsole.log(now.format('YYYY-MM-DD')); // 2025-03-27\\n\\n// An example of the usage of date-fns\\nimport { format, addDays } from 'date-fns';\\nconst today = new Date();\\nconst tomorrow = addDays(today, 1);\\nconsole.log(format(tomorrow, 'yyyy-MM-dd')); // 2025-03-28\\n\\n// An example of the usage of dayjs\\nimport dayjs from 'dayjs';\\nconst now = dayjs();\\nconsole.log(now.format('YYYY-MM-DD')); // 2025-03-27"],[2612,"To work with complex tables, libraries such as React Table or AG Grid are normally used. The filtering and the sorting can be performed on the client or on the server. The editing is implemented through the management of the rows' and the cells' state. It matters to organize the table's state and the handling of the changes properly.","The basic concepts\\nComplex tables require the management of the data's state, the filtering, the sorting and the editing. That is normally implemented by means of specialized libraries, which take on the logic of the rendering and of the handling of the events.\\n\\nThe filtering and the sorting\\nThe filtering can be client-side (for small sets of data) or server-side (for large volumes). The sorting is normally performed by one or by several columns. An example with React Table:\\n\\nThe editing\\nThe editing of cells or rows requires the management of the editing state. You can use inline editing, where a cell becomes an input field on a click. An example in React:\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of ready-made libraries for complex tables speeds the development up and reduces the number of bugs. They provide flexible APIs for the customization and the integration with any state management. It is recommended that you apply such solutions for tables with a large amount of data and with complex interaction logic.","import { useTable, useFilters, useSortBy } from 'react-table';\\n\\nfunction Table({ columns, data }) {\\n  const {\\n    getTableProps,\\n    getTableBodyProps,\\n    headerGroups,\\n    rows,\\n    prepareRow,\\n  } = useTable(\\n    { columns, data },\\n    useFilters,\\n    useSortBy\\n  );\\n\\n  return (\\n    <table {...getTableProps()}>\\n      <thead>\\n        {headerGroups.map(headerGroup => (\\n          <tr {...headerGroup.getHeaderGroupProps()}>\\n            {headerGroup.headers.map(column => (\\n              <th {...column.getHeaderProps(column.getSortByToggleProps())}>\\n                {column.render('Header')}\\n                <span>{column.isSorted ? (column.isSortedDesc ? ' 🔽' : ' 🔼') : ''}</span>\\n                <div>{column.canFilter ? column.render('Filter') : null}</div>\\n              </th>\\n            ))}\\n          </tr>\\n        ))}\\n      </thead>\\n      <tbody {...getTableBodyProps()}>\\n        {rows.map(row => {\\n          prepareRow(row);\\n          return (\\n            <tr {...row.getRowProps()}>\\n              {row.cells.map(cell => (\\n                <td {...cell.getCellProps()}>{cell.render('Cell')}</td>\\n              ))}\\n            </tr>\\n          );\\n        })}\\n      </tbody>\\n    </table>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction EditableCell({ value: initialValue, row, column, updateMyData }) {\\n  const [value, setValue] = React.useState(initialValue);\\n  const onBlur = () => updateMyData(row.index, column.id, value);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <input\\n      value={value}\\n      onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)}\\n      onBlur={onBlur}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}"],[2613,"Server state is the data held on the server, which requires asynchronous requests in order to be obtained or updated (a list of users, for example). Client state is the data that exists only in the browser and is not synchronized with the server (the UI's state, an input form, for example). The main difference is in the source of truth: server state is managed by the server, client state locally. For server state, libraries such as React Query or RTK Query are often used, and for client state Redux or the Context API.","The difference between server-side and client-side state management\\nIn modern web applications the data can be held both on the server and on the client. An understanding of the difference between those approaches is critically important for the application's architecture, for the performance and for the ease of maintenance.\\n\\nClient state\\nClient state is the data that exists only in the user's browser and requires no synchronization with the server. Examples:\\n- The currently selected item in a dropdown list\\n- A modal window's state (open/closed)\\n- A form's data, which has not yet been submitted\\n- The design theme (light/dark)\\n\\nTo manage client state, global stores such as Redux, Zustand or the Context API are often used. That data requires no asynchronous operations for reading and writing; it is available instantly.\\n\\nServer state\\nServer state is the data held on the server, which requires asynchronous requests (through a REST API or GraphQL, for example) in order to be obtained, updated or deleted. Examples:\\n- The list of products in an online shop\\n- A user's profile\\n- The history of orders\\n\\nServer state is not under the client's complete control: other users or background processes can change the data on the server. The client therefore has to be able to synchronize with the server, to handle the loading and error states, and to cache the data for optimization.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- The source of truth: for server state the server; for client state the local store.\\n- The synchronization: server state requires asynchronous requests and the handling of a cache; client state is synchronous.\\n- The management: server state is often managed by specialized libraries (React Query, SWR, RTK Query); client state by Redux, Zustand or Context.\\n- The caching: server state needs cache-invalidation strategies; client state does not.\\n\\nConclusion: the separation into server and client state helps you avoid redundant requests to the server and makes the architecture simpler. Use specialized libraries for server state, in order to automate the caching and the synchronization, and for client state lightweight solutions, which require no asynchrony.","// An example of client state with Redux Toolkit\\nconst uiSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'ui',\\n  initialState: { isModalOpen: false },\\n  reducers: {\\n    toggleModal(state) {\\n      state.isModalOpen = !state.isModalOpen;\\n    }\\n  }\\n});\\n// The change happens instantly, without requests to the server\\n\\n// An example of server state with React Query\\nfunction useProducts() {\\n  return useQuery({\\n    queryKey: ['products'],\\n    queryFn: () => fetch('/api/products').then(res => res.json()),\\n    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // the data counts as fresh for 5 minutes\\n  });\\n}\\n// The data is loaded from the server asynchronously and cached"],[2614,"The standard frontend stack includes HTML, CSS, JavaScript, frameworks (React, Vue, Angular), bundlers (Webpack, Vite) and package managers (npm, yarn). Telegram Mini Apps are web applications started inside Telegram, with limited access to the device's API but with the ability to interact with the user through a bot. The particulars: the use of the Telegram Web App SDK, working through an iframe, a limited size, and the need to adapt to mobile devices.","The frontend-development stack\\nThe typical frontend stack includes several key components. The basis is HTML, CSS and JavaScript, which provide the structure, the styling and the logic. To make the development easier, frameworks such as React, Vue.js or Angular are used. Module bundlers — Webpack or Vite, for example — help you combine the files and optimize the performance. Package managers (npm, yarn) manage the dependencies. CSS preprocessors (Sass, Less) and tools for testing (Jest, Cypress) are additionally applied.\\n\\nTelegram Mini Apps\\nTelegram Mini Apps are web applications that are started inside the Telegram messenger, through a special interface. They work in an iframe and interact with the user through a bot. The main particulars:\\n- The use of the Telegram Web App SDK for access to Telegram's functions (sending data, working with the keyboard, for example).\\n- A limited size of the application (up to 1 MB for the static files).\\n- The need to adapt to mobile devices, since most users come in through smartphones.\\n- The absence of direct access to the device's API (the camera, the geolocation) without the user's permission.\\n\\nAn example of a simple Mini App in React:\\n\\nIn this example the SDK is used for initializing the application and displaying a button. Mini Apps are often applied for games, surveys, shops or services, where fast access without an installation is required.\\n\\nConclusion: a knowledge of the frontend stack is necessary for creating modern web applications, while Telegram Mini Apps are a niche but growing area, where it matters to take the platform's limitations into account and to use the specialized SDKs.","import React, { useEffect } from 'react';\\nimport WebApp from '@twa-dev/sdk';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    WebApp.ready();\\n    WebApp.MainButton.setText('Click me');\\n    WebApp.MainButton.show();\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <h1>Hello, Telegram!</h1>;\\n}\\n\\nexport default App;"],[2615,"RxJS's operators are divided into several categories: creation operators (of, from, interval), transformation ones (map, switchMap, mergeMap), filtering ones (filter, take, debounceTime), combination ones (combineLatest, merge, forkJoin), plus error-handling operators (catchError, retry) and utilities (tap, delay). Every group solves its own task when working with Observables.","The main categories of RxJS's operators\\nRxJS's operators are functions that let you manipulate the streams of data created by Observables. They are divided into several groups, depending on the task they perform. An understanding of those categories helps you write clean, declarative code for asynchronous operations.\\n\\nThe creation operators\\nThese operators create new Observables from various sources: values, arrays, events or promises.\\n\\nThe transformation operators\\nThey change every value in the stream, or transform one Observable into another.\\n\\nThe filtering operators\\nThey let you pass only the values that satisfy a condition, or limit the number of values.\\n\\nThe combination operators\\nThey unite several Observables into one.\\n\\nThe error-handling operators and the utilities\\nThey let you handle errors and perform side effects.\\n\\nConclusion: RxJS's operators are a powerful tool for working with asynchronous streams. Their proper use lets you write declarative, easily testable, maintainable code, especially in applications with a large number of events, requests or streams of data.","import { of, from, interval } from 'rxjs';\\n\\nof(1, 2, 3).subscribe(console.log); // 1, 2, 3\\nfrom([4, 5, 6]).subscribe(console.log); // 4, 5, 6\\ninterval(1000).subscribe(console.log); // 0, 1, 2, ... every second\\n\\nimport { map, switchMap } from 'rxjs/operators';\\n\\nof(1, 2, 3).pipe(\\n  map(x => x * 10)\\n).subscribe(console.log); // 10, 20, 30\\n\\n// switchMap cancels the previous inner Observable\\nfromEvent(input, 'input').pipe(\\n  switchMap(value => fetchData(value))\\n).subscribe(console.log);\\n\\nimport { filter, take, debounceTime } from 'rxjs/operators';\\n\\nof(1, 2, 3, 4, 5).pipe(\\n  filter(x => x % 2 === 0),\\n  take(2)\\n).subscribe(console.log); // 2, 4\\n\\nfromEvent(input, 'input').pipe(\\n  debounceTime(300)\\n).subscribe(console.log); // it waits 300 ms after the last input\\n\\nimport { combineLatest, merge, forkJoin } from 'rxjs';\\n\\ncombineLatest([obs1$, obs2$]).subscribe(([val1, val2]) => {\\n  console.log(val1, val2);\\n});\\n\\nmerge(obs1$, obs2$).subscribe(console.log); // the values from both streams as they appear\\n\\nforkJoin({ a: obs1$, b: obs2$ }).subscribe(console.log); // it waits for all of them to complete\\n\\nimport { catchError, retry, tap } from 'rxjs/operators';\\n\\nhttp$.pipe(\\n  retry(3),\\n  catchError(err => of('fallback')),\\n  tap(value => console.log('Received:', value))\\n).subscribe(console.log);"],[2616,"A controlled input in React is an element whose value is held in the component's state and updated through an onChange handler. The input's value is taken from the state rather than from the DOM. That lets React control the input of the data completely. An example: \`<input value={value} onChange={e => setValue(e.target.value)} />\`.","What is a controlled input?\\nA controlled input is a form element whose value is managed by a React component's state. Rather than relying on the DOM's internal state, React takes on the management of the input field's value through the \`value\` property and the \`onChange\` handler. That makes the form's behaviour predictable and easy to test.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nOn every change of the text in the input, the \`onChange\` function is called, which updates the component's state. React then repaints the component, and the input receives the new value from the state. Thus the source of truth is React's state rather than the DOM.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere is it applied?\\nControlled components are used in forms where validation, the dynamic updating of other fields or the sending of the data to a server is required. They give complete control over the input, which makes implementing complex logic easier.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a controlled input when the field's value has to be managed through React — for validation or for integration with other components, for example. That is the standard approach in React applications.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction ControlledInput() {\\n  const [text, setText] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleChange = (event) => {\\n    setText(event.target.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <input\\n      type=\\"text\\"\\n      value={text}\\n      onChange={handleChange}\\n    />\\n  );\\n}"],[2617,"To obtain access to the value of an uncontrolled input in React, the useRef hook is used. Create a ref by means of useRef and pass it into the input element's ref attribute. The value can then be obtained through inputRef.current.value — when the form is submitted or on a click on a button, for example.","Uncontrolled components in React\\nIn React components can be controlled or uncontrolled. Uncontrolled components hold their state in the DOM rather than in React's state. That is useful when you do not have to track every change of the input but have to obtain the value only at a particular moment — when the form is submitted, for example.\\n\\nThe use of useRef\\nFor access to the DOM element of an uncontrolled input, the \`useRef\` hook is used. It creates a ref object, which can be bound to the element through the \`ref\` attribute. After that the input's value is available through \`ref.current.value\`.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the input is not controlled by React — its value is managed by the browser. When the form is submitted, we obtain the value through the ref.\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\nUncontrolled components with a ref are convenient for simple forms, for integration with non-React code, or when real-time validation is not required. For complex forms with dynamic validation, however, it is better to use controlled components.","import React, { useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    alert('The value: ' + inputRef.current.value);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>\\n      <input type=\\"text\\" ref={inputRef} defaultValue=\\"\\" />\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Send</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2618,"The key attribute helps React identify the elements of a list on an update. It lets React minimize the repaints by matching the old and the new elements. The array index can be used as the key only if the list is static and does not change. In dynamic lists that can lead to errors in the state and in the performance.","The purpose of the key attribute\\nThe \`key\` attribute in React is used for identifying every element in a list during the rendering. It helps the reconciliation algorithm update the DOM efficiently, by determining which elements have been added, deleted or changed. Without a unique key React may repaint the whole list, which reduces the performance.\\n\\nWhy is the array index a bad idea?\\nThe use of the array index as the key can lead to problems if the list is dynamic: elements are added, deleted or reordered. When an element is deleted from the middle of a list, for example, the indexes shift, and React may match the old and the new elements wrongly, which will cause errors in the state (the loss of the focus in an input, for example).\\n\\nWhen can the index be used?\\nThe index can be used only for static lists that do not change after the rendering (a list of constants, for example). In every other case it is better to apply unique identifiers from the data.\\n\\nConclusion: use unique, stable keys for dynamic lists, in order to avoid bugs and to ensure high performance of React applications.","// Bad: the index as the key\\n{items.map((item, index) => <li key={index}>{item}</li>)}\\n\\n// Good: a unique id\\n{items.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}"],[2619,"To work with styles I have used the preprocessors Sass and Less, the BEM methodology for naming the classes, CSS Modules for isolating the styles in components, and CSS-in-JS libraries such as styled-components. These tools help you organize the code, avoid conflicts and speed the development up.","The tools for working with styles\\nIn modern web development the styling goes beyond simple CSS. To improve the efficiency and the maintainability of the code, various tools and approaches are used. Let us look at the main ones.\\n\\nThe preprocessors\\nSass and Less are preprocessors, which extend CSS's capabilities. They add variables, nesting, mixins and functions, which makes the code more modular and more convenient to reuse.\\n\\nThe methodologies\\nBEM (Block Element Modifier) is a methodology for naming classes, which helps you structure your CSS and avoid conflicts. Every class describes an element's role: a block, an element or a modifier.\\n\\nCSS Modules\\nCSS Modules generate unique class names automatically, which prevents them intersecting in different components. That is especially useful in React applications.\\n\\nCSS-in-JS\\nLibraries such as styled-components let you write CSS directly inside JavaScript. That gives you the ability to use dynamic styles on the basis of the props and the state.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of tool depends on the project. For large projects with a team, Sass and BEM suit; for component-based architectures CSS Modules or styled-components. The main thing is to ensure the readability and the scalability of the styles.","// An example in Sass\\n$primary-color: #3498db;\\n\\n.button {\\n  background-color: $primary-color;\\n  &:hover {\\n    background-color: darken($primary-color, 10%);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n<div class=\\"card card--featured\\">\\n  <h2 class=\\"card__title\\">A heading</h2>\\n  <p class=\\"card__text\\">Some text</p>\\n</div>\\n\\n// styles.module.css\\n.button {\\n  background: blue;\\n}\\n\\n// Component.jsx\\nimport styles from './styles.module.css';\\n<button className={styles.button}>Click</button>\\n\\nimport styled from 'styled-components';\\n\\nconst Button = styled.button\`\\n  background: \${props => props.primary ? 'blue' : 'gray'};\\n  color: white;\\n\`;"],[2620,"A custom useFetch hook encapsulates the logic of an HTTP request. It returns the data, a loading flag and an error object. Use useState for holding the states and useEffect for performing the request when the URL changes. That makes the reuse of the code easier and makes the components cleaner.","What useFetch is, and what it is for\\nA custom useFetch hook is a function that wraps the logic of performing an HTTP request and of managing its states (loading, success, error). Rather than duplicating code with fetch, useState and useEffect in every component, you create one reusable hook. That improves the readability, the testability and the maintainability of the code.\\n\\nHow do you implement useFetch?\\nThe hook takes a URL and optional request parameters. Inside it it uses useState for holding the data, the loading state and the error, and useEffect for performing the request when the URL changes. It matters to handle the cancellation of the request (the cleanup), in order to avoid memory leaks and an update of the state after the component has unmounted.\\n\\nHow do you use the hook in a component?\\nSimply call useFetch with the URL you need and destructure the values returned. Depending on the states, you can show a spinner, an error message or display the data.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA custom useFetch hook is a powerful pattern for centralizing the logic of HTTP requests in React applications. It reduces the duplication of code, improves the readability and makes testing easier. Use it everywhere data has to be obtained from an API, especially in large projects with a great many requests.","import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction useFetch(url, options = {}) {\\n  const [data, setData] = useState(null);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);\\n  const [error, setError] = useState(null);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    let cancelled = false;\\n    setLoading(true);\\n    setError(null);\\n\\n    fetch(url, options)\\n      .then(res => {\\n        if (!res.ok) throw new Error(\`HTTP error! status: \${res.status}\`);\\n        return res.json();\\n      })\\n      .then(json => {\\n        if (!cancelled) {\\n          setData(json);\\n          setLoading(false);\\n        }\\n      })\\n      .catch(err => {\\n        if (!cancelled) {\\n          setError(err.message);\\n          setLoading(false);\\n        }\\n      });\\n\\n    return () => { cancelled = true; };\\n  }, [url]);\\n\\n  return { data, loading, error };\\n}\\n\\nfunction UserList() {\\n  const { data, loading, error } = useFetch('https://api.example.com/users');\\n\\n  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  if (error) return <div>Error: {error}</div>;\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {data.map(user => <li key={user.id}>{user.name}</li>)}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2621,"An optimistic update is a technique in which the interface is updated immediately after the user's action, without waiting for the server's response. That makes the application more responsive. To indicate a background update, you can use spinners, toasts or timestamps. If the request ends in an error, the changes are rolled back and a notification is shown to the user.","What are optimistic updates?\\nAn optimistic update is an approach in which the user interface (the UI) is updated immediately after an action is performed, on the assumption that the request to the server will succeed. That improves the perception of the application's speed, since the user does not wait for the response from the server. If the request ends in an error, however, the changes have to be rolled back and the user notified.\\n\\nHow do you give an indication of a background update?\\nTo indicate a background update you can use several methods:\\n- Spinners or progress bars — they show that an operation is in progress.\\n- Toasts or notifications — they report the success or the error once it has completed.\\n- Timestamps — they show when the data was last updated.\\n- The blocking of repeat actions — it prevents repeat submissions before the request completes.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation in React\\n\\nConclusion\\nOptimistic updates are worth applying in applications where the responsiveness of the interface matters — in social networks or messengers, for example. An indication of the background update helps the user understand the system's state and avoid confusion when errors are possible.","import { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TodoApp() {\\n  const [todos, setTodos] = useState([]);\\n  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);\\n\\n  const addTodo = async (text) => {\\n    const optimisticTodo = { id: Date.now(), text, completed: false };\\n    setTodos(prev => [...prev, optimisticTodo]);\\n    setLoading(true);\\n\\n    try {\\n      const response = await fetch('/api/todos', {\\n        method: 'POST',\\n        body: JSON.stringify({ text }),\\n      });\\n      if (!response.ok) throw new Error('Failed');\\n      const savedTodo = await response.json();\\n      setTodos(prev => prev.map(t => t.id === optimisticTodo.id ? savedTodo : t));\\n    } catch {\\n      setTodos(prev => prev.filter(t => t.id !== optimisticTodo.id));\\n      alert('An error while adding the task');\\n    } finally {\\n      setLoading(false);\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      {loading && <span>Saving...</span>}\\n      <ul>{todos.map(t => <li key={t.id}>{t.text}</li>)}</ul>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2622,"SWR uses the stale-while-revalidate strategy, which can lead to stale data being displayed while the update happens. Problems arise with frequent mutations of the data, when the cache does not manage to update in time, or when there is no revalidation. Conflicts during parallel requests and the incorrect working of optimistic updates are also possible.","The problems of the currency of the data in SWR\\nSWR (stale-while-revalidate) is a caching strategy in which the cached (possibly stale) data is shown first and then a request for an update is performed in the background. That can lead to situations in which the user sees information that is not current.\\n\\nThe main nuances\\n\\n- A delay in the update: if the data changes often, the user can see stale data until the revalidation completes.\\n- The absence of automatic revalidation: by default SWR does not update the data if the window is not in focus or the time stated has not passed. That can lead to stale data being displayed for a long while.\\n- Conflicts during mutations: with optimistic updates (when the UI is updated before the server's response), discrepancies are possible if the server returns an error or different data.\\n- Problems with parallel requests: if several components request the same data, SWR can create several requests, which increases the load and can lead to inconsistency.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nHow to minimize the problems\\n\\n- Configure \`refreshInterval\` for a periodic update.\\n- Use \`mutate\` for a manual update after mutations.\\n- Switch \`revalidateOnFocus\` and \`revalidateOnReconnect\` on.\\n- For critical data, use \`dedupingInterval\` in order to prevent duplicate requests.\\n\\nConclusion: SWR is convenient for caching and for the fast display of data, but it requires careful configuration of the validation parameters and the handling of the mutations, in order to avoid problems with the currency. Apply it in projects where a small delay in the updating of the data is permissible, and combine it with manual synchronization for the critical operations.","import useSWR from 'swr';\\n\\nfunction Profile() {\\n  const { data, error } = useSWR('/api/user', fetcher, {\\n    revalidateOnFocus: true,\\n    refreshInterval: 5000, // an update every 5 seconds\\n  });\\n\\n  if (error) return <div>A loading error</div>;\\n  if (!data) return <div>Loading...</div>;\\n  return <div>Hello, {data.name}</div>;\\n}"],[2623,"To type components with HTML attributes, use interfaces such as React.HTMLAttributes, or extend them. Refs are typed through React.RefObject, or through useRef with the element's type stated. That ensures type safety when working with the DOM and with the props.","The typing of components with HTML attributes and refs\\nIn React, when creating components that wrap native HTML elements, it matters to type the props properly, in order to preserve the support for the standard attributes (className, id, onClick, for example). TypeScript provides built-in types such as React.HTMLAttributes and React.InputHTMLAttributes, which can be extended for adding custom props. Refs are used for direct access to DOM elements and have to be typed by means of React.RefObject or React.MutableRefObject.\\n\\nAn example of typing a component with HTML attributes\\nSuppose we are creating a CustomInput component, which takes all of the standard attributes of an input plus an additional label prop. We use an intersection of types:\\n\\nNow the component takes all of the input's attributes (placeholder, value, onChange and so on) plus the label.\\n\\nThe typing of refs\\nRefs are typed by means of the generic in useRef. For access to an input, for example:\\n\\nThe HTMLInputElement type guarantees we can call the methods specific to an input (focus, select).\\n\\nThe passing of refs into child components\\nTo pass a ref into a functional component, use React.forwardRef:\\n\\nThat lets the parent component manage the focus or the value of the child input.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe typing of HTML attributes and refs in React with TypeScript improves the reliability of the code, by preventing type errors when working with the DOM. Use React's built-in types and forwardRef for the flexible passing of refs. That is especially useful in large projects with a great many UI components.","import React from 'react';\\n\\ninterface CustomInputProps extends React.InputHTMLAttributes<HTMLInputElement> {\\n  label: string;\\n}\\n\\nconst CustomInput: React.FC<CustomInputProps> = ({ label, ...inputProps }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <label>{label}</label>\\n      <input {...inputProps} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nimport React, { useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nconst MyComponent: React.FC = () => {\\n  const inputRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\\n\\n  const focusInput = () => {\\n    inputRef.current?.focus();\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <input ref={inputRef} type=\\"text\\" />\\n      <button onClick={focusInput}>Focus</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nconst FancyInput = React.forwardRef<HTMLInputElement, { placeholder: string }>(\\n  (props, ref) => {\\n    return <input ref={ref} placeholder={props.placeholder} />;\\n  }\\n);\\n\\n// The usage\\nconst parentRef = useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);\\n<FancyInput ref={parentRef} placeholder=\\"Type here\\" />;"],[2624,"If a component's type has changed (from a div to a span, for example, or from one class to another), React unmounts the old component completely and mounts a new one, even if the same key is stated. The state is not preserved, since these are different types. A key helps preserve the state only when components of the same type are rearranged or updated.","React's behaviour when a component's type changes\\nReact uses the component's type as the main identifier for the comparison during the reconciliation. If an element's type changes (a \`<div>\` is replaced with a \`<span>\`, for example, or one functional component with another), React considers that these are completely different elements and performs a complete unmounting of the old one and a mounting of the new one. That means all of the local state, the effects and the DOM nodes of the previous component are destroyed.\\n\\nThe role of key\\nThe \`key\` attribute helps React identify elements among components of the same type (in a list, for example). When the component's type changes, however, the key does not affect React's decision: the type has priority. Even if the same key is stated, React will recreate the component anyway, since the types are different.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nWhen does key help\\nA key is effective when the component's type does not change but its position in a list does, or when elements are added or removed. When sorting an array of components of the same type, for example, the key lets React preserve every element's state by matching them by key.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA change of a component's type always leads to the loss of the state, independently of the key. A key is useful only for components of the same type in dynamic lists. Use a key for the stable identification of elements of the same type, in order to avoid an unnecessary recreation.","// Component A and B are different types\\nfunction A() { return <div>A</div>; }\\nfunction B() { return <div>B</div>; }\\n\\n// When switching from A to B the state will not be preserved\\n{condition ? <A key=\\"same\\" /> : <B key=\\"same\\" />}"],[2625,"React uses a shallow comparison in order to determine changes of the props. It compares the references to the objects plus the primitive values. If the reference to an object has not changed, React considers the props the same. That lets you avoid redundant re-renders when using React.memo or PureComponent.","How React compares props\\nReact uses a shallow comparison for checking the changes of the props. That means only the references to the objects and the primitive values are compared rather than their content. If a prop is an object or an array, React checks whether the reference to that object has changed rather than its internal fields.\\n\\nAn example of a shallow comparison\\nLet us look at a component wrapped in React.memo:\\n\\nHow this works\\nReact.memo and PureComponent implement the shouldComponentUpdate method, which performs a shallow comparison of all of the props. If all of the props have stayed the same (by reference), the component does not re-render. That is especially useful for optimizing the performance in large lists or with frequent updates of the parent component.\\n\\nThe practical application\\nA shallow comparison is effective for the primitive types (numbers, strings, booleans) and for immutable objects. If you pass objects that change often, it is recommended that you use useMemo or useCallback in order to stabilize the references:\\n\\nConclusion\\nA shallow comparison is a fast, efficient way of avoiding redundant re-renders in React. It is worth applying in components that repaint often but receive stable props. For deep objects, use the stabilization of the references through hooks or immutability libraries.","const MyComponent = React.memo(({ data }) => {\\n  console.log('render');\\n  return <div>{data.value}</div>;\\n});\\n\\n// With such a call the component does not re-render:\\nconst data = { value: 1 };\\n<MyComponent data={data} />\\n<MyComponent data={data} /> // the reference is the same\\n\\n// But with this one it does re-render:\\n<MyComponent data={{ value: 1 }} />\\n<MyComponent data={{ value: 1 }} /> // different references\\n\\nconst memoizedData = useMemo(() => ({ value: 1 }), []);\\n<MyComponent data={memoizedData} />"],[2626,"In React a controlled component is used for implementing such a form: the select's value is held in the state, and on its basis the additional fields are rendered conditionally. When the select changes, the state updates, which causes a repaint with the fields needed. That lets you change the form's structure flexibly without manipulating the DOM directly.","Dynamic forms with conditional rendering\\nWhen a form has to change depending on the user's choice, the approach of controlled components plus conditional rendering is used. The state holds the select's current value, and on its basis the corresponding input fields are displayed.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\nLet us look at a form where, when 'email' is chosen, a field for an email appears, and when 'phone' is chosen, a field for a phone number with a mask.\\n\\nThe key points\\n\\n- The form's state is held in the React component, which makes it predictable.\\n- When the select changes, the state updates, and React repaints only the parts that have changed.\\n- It matters to reset the dependent fields when the type changes, in order to avoid incorrect data.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSuch an approach is applied in complex forms, questionnaires and settings, where the set of fields depends on the previous choice. It ensures the cleanliness of the code and ease of maintenance.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction DynamicForm() {\\n  const [contactType, setContactType] = useState('email');\\n  const [contactValue, setContactValue] = useState('');\\n\\n  const handleTypeChange = (e) => {\\n    setContactType(e.target.value);\\n    setContactValue(''); // the reset of the value when the type changes\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form>\\n      <select value={contactType} onChange={handleTypeChange}>\\n        <option value=\\"email\\">Email</option>\\n        <option value=\\"phone\\">Phone</option>\\n      </select>\\n      {contactType === 'email' ? (\\n        <input\\n          type=\\"email\\"\\n          value={contactValue}\\n          onChange={(e) => setContactValue(e.target.value)}\\n          placeholder=\\"Enter email\\"\\n        />\\n      ) : (\\n        <input\\n          type=\\"tel\\"\\n          value={contactValue}\\n          onChange={(e) => setContactValue(e.target.value)}\\n          placeholder=\\"Enter phone\\"\\n        />\\n      )}\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}"],[2627,"React Native provides APIs for working with the native capabilities, through built-in modules and third-party libraries. For access to the file system react-native-fs is used; for the camera react-native-camera or expo-camera. Permissions have to be requested through PermissionsAndroid or the Permissions API. These modules let you perform operations that are unavailable in the web while preserving a single codebase.","Work with the native capabilities in React Native\\nReact Native lets you interact with a device's native functions, such as the file system, the camera, the geolocation and others, through the system of native modules. That is achieved by means of a bridge, which links the JavaScript code to the native iOS and Android APIs. To make the development easier, popular third-party libraries are used, which provide ready-made interfaces.\\n\\nThe file system\\nFor working with the file system the \`react-native-fs\` library is applied. It lets you read, write and delete files and manage directories. An example of writing a file:\\n\\nThe camera\\nFor access to the camera, \`react-native-camera\` or \`expo-camera\` is used (if the project is on Expo). The library provides an \`RNCamera\` component, which can be embedded in the interface. An example:\\n\\nThe permissions\\nFor access to the native functions, permissions have to be requested. On Android \`PermissionsAndroid\` is used; on iOS \`Permissions\` from the \`react-native-permissions\` library. An example of a request for the camera permission on Android:\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of native modules in React Native lets you create applications with full access to a device's capabilities while preserving a single codebase for iOS and Android. That is especially useful for applications that require work with files, media or sensors, where the web technologies are limited.","import RNFS from 'react-native-fs';\\n\\nconst path = \`\${RNFS.DocumentDirectoryPath}/example.txt\`;\\nRNFS.writeFile(path, 'Hello, World!', 'utf8')\\n  .then(() => console.log('File written'))\\n  .catch(err => console.error(err));\\n\\nimport { RNCamera } from 'react-native-camera';\\n\\nconst CameraView = () => (\\n  <RNCamera\\n    style={{ flex: 1 }}\\n    type={RNCamera.Constants.Type.back}\\n    captureAudio={false}\\n  />\\n);\\n\\nimport { PermissionsAndroid } from 'react-native';\\n\\nasync function requestCameraPermission() {\\n  try {\\n    const granted = await PermissionsAndroid.request(\\n      PermissionsAndroid.PERMISSIONS.CAMERA\\n    );\\n    if (granted === PermissionsAndroid.RESULTS.GRANTED) {\\n      console.log('Camera permission granted');\\n    }\\n  } catch (err) {\\n    console.warn(err);\\n  }\\n}"],[2628,"To build a React Native application you have to configure the signing: for Android create a keystore and state it in gradle.properties; for iOS configure the certificates and the provisioning profile in Xcode. Then run the commands: for Android cd android && ./gradlew bundleRelease, for iOS npx react-native run-ios --configuration Release. After the build you can upload the build to the Google Play Console or to App Store Connect.","The general process of building and signing a React Native application\\nBuilding a React Native application for a release includes several stages: the configuration of the signing (code signing), the configuration of the build, and the generation of the final artefact (an APK/AAB for Android, an IPA for iOS). The signing is necessary for identifying the developer and for ensuring the application's integrity.\\n\\nAndroid: the keystore and Gradle\\nFor Android you have to create a keystore — a file with the signing key. Create it through keytool:\\n\\nPut the file in the android/app folder. Then in the file android/gradle.properties add:\\n\\nIn android/app/build.gradle configure the signingConfigs:\\n\\nBuild the AAB (recommended for Google Play):\\n\\nThe APK can be obtained with the command ./gradlew assembleRelease.\\n\\niOS: the certificates and the provisioning profile\\nFor iOS the Apple Developer Program is required. In Xcode: choose the project, the Signing & Capabilities tab, switch Automatically manage signing on (or configure it by hand). Make sure you have a Distribution Certificate and an App Store Provisioning Profile. Build the archive:\\n\\nOr through Xcode: Product > Archive. After the archiving the Organizer will open, where you can upload the build to App Store Connect.\\n\\nThe preparation for the release\\nBefore the build, update the version in package.json and configure the icons and the splash screen. For Android also configure the versionCode and the versionName in android/app/build.gradle. For iOS, in Info.plist and in Xcode's General settings.\\n\\nConclusion: the proper configuration of the signing and the build is a critical stage before the publication. Use automatic management of the signing for iOS and keep the keystore safe for Android. That will ensure a successful upload to the app stores.","keytool -genkey -v -keystore my-release-key.keystore -alias my-key-alias -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000\\n\\nMYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_FILE=my-release-key.keystore\\nMYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_ALIAS=my-key-alias\\nMYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_PASSWORD=*****\\nMYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_PASSWORD=*****\\n\\nsigningConfigs {\\n    release {\\n        storeFile file(MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_FILE)\\n        storePassword MYAPP_UPLOAD_STORE_PASSWORD\\n        keyAlias MYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_ALIAS\\n        keyPassword MYAPP_UPLOAD_KEY_PASSWORD\\n    }\\n}\\nbuildTypes {\\n    release {\\n        signingConfig signingConfigs.release\\n    }\\n}\\n\\ncd android && ./gradlew bundleRelease\\n\\nnpx react-native run-ios --configuration Release"],[2629,"First the console.log in the parent component's body runs, then the one in the child's body. Once the DOM has mounted, the useEffects are called: first the parent's, then the child's. That is because the rendering of the components happens from the top down, while the effects go from the bottom up once the rendering has completed.","The order in which console.log runs in React\\nWhen React renders components, it first runs the code in the component function's body (including the console.log) for the parent and then for the child components. Once all of the components have been rendered and the DOM has been updated, React starts the useEffect hooks in the order from the children to the parents.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe order of the output\\n\\n- Parent render\\n- Child render\\n- Child effect\\n- Parent effect\\n\\nThat is explained by the fact that the rendering goes from the top down, while the effects run once all of the components have mounted, starting with the deepest.\\n\\nConclusion: an understanding of this order matters for debugging and for the proper use of useEffect, especially when working with side effects that depend on the child components.","function Parent() {\\n  console.log('Parent render');\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Parent effect');\\n  }, []);\\n  return <Child />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Child() {\\n  console.log('Child render');\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Child effect');\\n  }, []);\\n  return null;\\n}"],[2630,"React uses the declarative approach. You describe what the UI should look like depending on the state, and React takes care of updating the DOM itself. In the imperative approach you write step-by-step instructions on how to change the interface. Declarative code is simpler to read, to test and to maintain.","The declarative vs the imperative approach in React\\nReact is built on the declarative approach. That means the developer describes the desired state of the interface, while React takes on the task of achieving it. Rather than writing a sequence of commands for changing the DOM, you simply declare what the UI should look like with particular data.\\n\\nAn example of a comparison\\nLet us look at the task: show a list of items and highlight the active one.\\n\\nThe imperative approach (in plain JavaScript):\\n\\nThe declarative approach (React):\\n\\nIn React you simply describe the UI's structure on the basis of the props and the state. React itself computes the difference between the previous and the new virtual DOM and updates the real DOM efficiently.\\n\\nThe advantages of the declarative approach\\n\\n- The code is more predictable and easier to debug\\n- There is less chance of introducing bugs through the manual management of the DOM\\n- It makes the reuse of components easier\\n- It lets you concentrate on the application's logic rather than on the details of updating the interface\\n\\nConclusion: React's declarative approach makes the development of UIs more productive and less prone to bugs. It is worth applying in any project where the maintainability and the scalability of the interface matter.","const list = document.getElementById('list');\\nconst items = ['A', 'B', 'C'];\\nitems.forEach((text, index) => {\\n  const li = document.createElement('li');\\n  li.textContent = text;\\n  if (index === activeIndex) {\\n    li.classList.add('active');\\n  }\\n  list.appendChild(li);\\n});\\n\\nfunction ItemList({ items, activeIndex }) {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {items.map((text, index) => (\\n        <li key={index} className={index === activeIndex ? 'active' : ''}>\\n          {text}\\n        </li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2631,"If the memoization of the individual cards is impossible, use the virtualization of the list (react-window, for example) for rendering only the visible elements. You can also apply useMemo for the filtering or the sorting of the data, and wrap the list component itself in React.memo. If the data updates too often, consider a debounce or a throttle for reducing the number of updates.","The problem of redundant repaints\\nWhen the data in the store (Redux or Zustand, for example) is updated by an external application, a re-render of the whole list of cards happens every time. If the memoization of the individual cards is impossible (because of dynamic props or complex logic, for example), you have to optimize at the level of the list.\\n\\nThe main strategies\\n\\n- The virtualization of the list: libraries such as react-window or react-virtualized render only the visible elements. That reduces the number of DOM nodes and repaints radically.\\n- React.memo for the list: wrap the list component in React.memo, so that it repaints only when its props change. If the props are an array of objects, use useMemo in order to stabilize the reference.\\n- A debounce/throttle: if the data updates more often than the UI needs, limit the frequency of the updates by means of a debounce (lodash.debounce, for example).\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nWhen the memoization of the individual cards is impossible, use virtualization in combination with React.memo and useMemo for stabilizing the data. That is especially useful for large lists (100+ items) with frequent updates from external sources.","import { useMemo } from 'react';\\nimport { FixedSizeList } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst CardList = ({ items }) => {\\n  // We stabilize the reference to the array\\n  const stableItems = useMemo(() => items, [items.length]);\\n\\n  const Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n    <div style={style}>\\n      <Card data={stableItems[index]} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n\\n  return (\\n    <FixedSizeList\\n      height={600}\\n      itemCount={stableItems.length}\\n      itemSize={120}\\n    >\\n      {Row}\\n    </FixedSizeList>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport default React.memo(CardList);"],[2632,"The Virtual DOM is not tied to the real DOM tree. createPortal lets you render child elements in another DOM node, but the Virtual DOM still manages them as part of the parent component. React updates the portal through its own reconciliation mechanism, ignoring the real DOM's hierarchy.","How the Virtual DOM works with createPortal\\nIn React the Virtual DOM is an abstract representation of the user interface, which does not depend on the real DOM structure. When you use \`createPortal\`, you tell React that a particular element should be mounted in another DOM container, but in the Virtual DOM it stays part of the parent component.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example \`Modal\` is rendered in \`#modal-root\`, but in the Virtual DOM it stays a child element of \`App\`. React manages the portal's updates through its own comparison algorithm (reconciliation), which works at the level of the Virtual DOM rather than of the real DOM.\\n\\nThe key aspects\\n\\n- The Virtual DOM does not change its hierarchy when portals are used — the portal stays part of the parent component in the Virtual DOM tree.\\n- React updates a portal in the same way as ordinary elements: when the state or the props change, it repaints only the parts needed.\\n- React's events and context (the Context API, for example) work through the Virtual DOM, so a portal can receive the context from the parent component even though it is physically in another place in the DOM.\\n\\nConclusion\\ncreatePortal is a powerful tool for rendering modal windows, tooltips or other elements outside the main flow of the DOM, while the Virtual DOM preserves the application's logical structure, which makes managing the state and the events easier.","import { createPortal } from 'react-dom';\\n\\nfunction Modal({ children }) {\\n  return createPortal(\\n    <div className=\\"modal\\">{children}</div>,\\n    document.getElementById('modal-root')\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <h1>Main App</h1>\\n      <Modal>\\n        <p>This is in a portal</p>\\n      </Modal>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2633,"Yes, it can. For that you have to wrap the functional component in forwardRef and use the useImperativeHandle hook inside, which lets you state exactly what will be available through the ref. You can return an object with the methods or the data needed, for example. That gives you control over the component's public API.","How a ref works with functional components\\nBy default functional components do not support a ref, since they have no class instance. React provides a mechanism for customizing a ref, however, by means of the combination of \`forwardRef\` and \`useImperativeHandle\`.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation\\n\\nHow this works\\n\\n- \`forwardRef\` passes the ref from the parent into the child component as the second argument.\\n- \`useImperativeHandle\` determines which properties and methods will be available through the ref.\\n- In the parent you can call those methods as if it were an instance of a class component.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`useImperativeHandle\` with \`forwardRef\` when an external interface has to be provided for a functional component — for managing the focus or animations, or for calling internal methods from the parent, for example.","import React, { forwardRef, useImperativeHandle, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = forwardRef((props, ref) => {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useImperativeHandle(ref, () => ({\\n    increment: () => setCount(c => c + 1),\\n    getCount: () => count\\n  }));\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const childRef = useRef();\\n\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <Child ref={childRef} />\\n      <button onClick={() => childRef.current.increment()}>+</button>\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2634,"forwardRef is a function in React, which lets you pass a ref from a parent component to a child one. It is used when you need access to a DOM element inside a child component — for the focus, for an animation or for integration with libraries, for example. Without forwardRef a ref is not forwarded through a component, since it is not a prop.","What is forwardRef?\\nforwardRef is a function from the React library, which lets a component take a ref and pass it on, normally to a DOM element or to another component. By default a ref is not passed through the props, so forwardRef is used for forwarding it.\\n\\nWhen is that needed?\\n\\n- Managing the focus on an input inside a custom component.\\n- Integration with libraries that require direct access to the DOM (animations or third-party UI libraries, for example).\\n- Measuring an element's size or position.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nA component wrapped in forwardRef receives a second argument — the ref. That ref can be bound to any DOM element inside the component. The parent uses useRef() and passes it in as the ref prop. React forwards the ref through forwardRef automatically.\\n\\nConclusion\\nforwardRef is useful when access to a DOM element inside a wrapper component has to be preserved without breaking the encapsulation. Use it for the focus, for animations or for integration with libraries that require direct access to the DOM.","import React, { forwardRef, useRef } from 'react';\\n\\nconst CustomInput = forwardRef((props, ref) => {\\n  return <input ref={ref} {...props} />;\\n});\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  const inputRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const focusInput = () => {\\n    inputRef.current.focus();\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <CustomInput ref={inputRef} placeholder=\\"Type here\\" />\\n      <button onClick={focusInput}>Focus Input</button>\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2635,"In FSD a sample is used for linking events and stores without direct dependencies between the modules. Subscriptions to events are organized through watch or sample inside the features or the entities, in order to avoid circular dependencies. That lets you preserve the isolation of the layers and makes testing easier.","The organization of samples and subscriptions in Effector with FSD\\nFeature-Sliced Design (FSD) assumes a strict hierarchy of layers: shared, entities, features, widgets, pages, app. In Effector it matters that the events and the stores do not create circular dependencies between the layers. A sample is used for that, which lets you link the sources with the target declaratively, without a direct import.\\n\\nAn example of the usage of sample\\nSuppose that in entities/user there is a store $user and an event fetchUserFx. In features/auth we want to update the user on a successful authorization. Rather than calling fetchUserFx from features directly, we use a sample:\\n\\nHere the sample subscribes to the successful completion of loginFx and starts fetchUserFx. That preserves the direction of the dependency: features -> entities rather than the other way round.\\n\\nSubscriptions through watch\\nFor debugging or side effects you can use watch, but in FSD it is recommended that watch be avoided inside the business logic, since it can lead to implicit links. It is better to use a sample for explicit links.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of sample in FSD helps you maintain a clean architecture, by avoiding circular dependencies and making the links between the modules explicit. That makes refactoring and testing easier, especially in large projects.","// features/auth/model.ts\\nimport { createEvent, sample } from 'effector';\\nimport { $user, fetchUserFx } from 'entities/user';\\n\\nexport const loginFx = createEffect(async (data) => {\\n  // the authorization logic\\n});\\n\\nsample({\\n  clock: loginFx.done,\\n  target: fetchUserFx,\\n});\\n\\n// An example of watch for logging\\nimport { $user } from 'entities/user';\\n\\n$user.watch((user) => {\\n  console.log('User updated:', user);\\n});"],[2636,"The slow loading of an SPA on a cold start is normally caused by a large bundle size, the absence of caching and unoptimized resources. The solution includes code splitting, the lazy loading of the modules, the minification and compression of the files, and the use of server-side rendering (SSR) for speeding the first paint up.","The causes of the slow loading of an SPA on a cold start\\nThe main problem is a large single JavaScript bundle, which is downloaded and run before the content is displayed. On a cold start (the first visit, or after the cache has been cleared) the browser downloads all of the resources from scratch, which increases the time to interactive (the TTI). The additional factors: unoptimized images, the absence of compression, render-blocking scripts.\\n\\nThe methods of optimization\\n\\n- Code Splitting — the breaking of the bundle into chunks by means of dynamic imports. In React Router, for example:\\n\\n- Lazy Loading — the deferred loading of images and of components that are not visible in the initial window.\\n- Compression and minification — the use of Gzip/Brotli on the server, the removal of superfluous whitespace and comments.\\n- Server-side rendering (SSR) — the generation of HTML on the server for a fast first paint (Next.js, Nuxt.js).\\n- Caching — the configuration of the Cache-Control HTTP headers and of a Service Worker for caching the static files.\\n\\nConclusion\\nApply code splitting and lazy loading in order to reduce the initial bundle, use SSR for the critical content, and configure the caching. That is especially useful for large SPAs with a great many routes and modules.","const Home = React.lazy(() => import('./Home'));\\nconst About = React.lazy(() => import('./About'));\\n\\n<Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n  <Routes>\\n    <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n    <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n  </Routes>\\n</Suspense>"],[2637,"React.memo prevents a repeat render of a component if its props have not changed. useCallback memoizes a function, so that its reference stays stable between renders. useMemo memoizes the result of a computation. When a function is passed into a component wrapped in React.memo, then without a useCallback every render of the parent creates a new function, which breaks the child component's memoization.","How useMemo, useCallback and React.memo work\\nReact.memo is a HOC (Higher-Order Component), which remembers the result of a component's render and repaints it only when the props change. It uses a shallow comparison of the previous and the new props. If the props have not changed, React.memo returns the cached result, avoiding a redundant render.\\n\\nuseCallback is a hook, which returns a memoized version of a function. It takes a function plus an array of dependencies. As long as the dependencies do not change, useCallback returns the very same reference to the function. That is critical, because in JavaScript every new function is a new object in memory.\\n\\nuseMemo is a hook for memoizing the results of computations. It takes a computing function plus an array of dependencies. The result of the computation is cached and recomputed only when the dependencies change.\\n\\nWhy it matters to wrap functions in useCallback when passing them into React.memo\\nWhen a parent component repaints, all of the functions declared inside it are created afresh. If you pass such a function as a prop into a child component wrapped in React.memo, then on every render of the parent the child component receives a new reference to the function. React.memo, on its shallow comparison, sees that the prop has changed (the old reference !== the new reference) and repaints the child component, despite the fact that the function's logic has not changed.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nIn this example, without the useCallback every click on Increment would cause a repaint of Child, even though onClick does not change. With the useCallback the reference is stable, and Child does not repaint needlessly.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse useCallback for the functions passed into components wrapped in React.memo, in order to preserve the benefits of the memoization. That matters especially in large lists, in complex forms or in components with a heavy render. You should not wrap every function, however — apply the optimization only where it really is necessary for the performance.","import React, { useState, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // Without a useCallback: every render creates a new function\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    console.log('Clicked');\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n      <Child onClick={handleClick} />\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2638,"Props in React are immutable and cannot change by themselves. They are passed from the parent component and change only on a re-render of it. To make a component render only when the props change, use React.memo for functional components or shouldComponentUpdate for class ones. These methods perform a shallow comparison of the props and prevent redundant re-renders.","The immutability of the props\\nProps in React are immutable data, which are passed from a parent component to a child one. They cannot change inside the component itself — that is a fundamental rule of React. If the props look as if they have changed, that means the parent component has performed a re-render and passed new values in.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nThe immutability guarantees the predictability of the data flow and makes debugging easier. A component always receives the props as its input data and should not modify them. If the data has to be changed, that is done through the state or through callbacks passed in the props.\\n\\nThe control of the re-renders\\nBy default React re-renders a component on any change of the parent's state or props. To optimize the performance and render a component only when its own props change, use:\\n- React.memo — for functional components. It remembers the result of the render and performs a shallow comparison of the props.\\n- shouldComponentUpdate — for class components. It is a lifecycle method, which returns true or false, stating whether the component has to be updated.\\n\\nAn example with React.memo\\n\\nIn this example \`ChildComponent\` is wrapped in \`React.memo\`. On a press of the button only the \`count\` state in the parent changes, while the \`name\` prop stays the same. Thanks to the \`React.memo\` the child component does not re-render, as can be seen from the absence of a message in the console.\\n\\nAn example with shouldComponentUpdate\\n\\nHere \`shouldComponentUpdate\` compares the current \`name\` prop with the new one. If they are equal, the method returns \`false\` and no re-render happens.\\n\\nConclusion\\nProps never change by themselves — they are immutable data. To prevent redundant re-renders, use \`React.memo\` or \`shouldComponentUpdate\`. These tools are especially useful in large applications with deeply nested components, where the optimization of the performance is critical.","import React from 'react';\\n\\nconst ChildComponent = React.memo(({ name }) => {\\n  console.log('Child rendered');\\n  return <div>Hello, {name}!</div>;\\n});\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = React.useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <ChildComponent name=\\"Alice\\" />\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nclass ChildComponent extends React.Component {\\n  shouldComponentUpdate(nextProps) {\\n    return nextProps.name !== this.props.name;\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    console.log('Child rendered');\\n    return <div>Hello, {this.props.name}!</div>;\\n  }\\n}"],[2639,"A React component's state does not depend on its placement in the DOM. Even if the component is rendered through a portal in another DOM node, its state stays bound to the logical tree of React components. A portal affects only where the HTML is displayed, not the component's lifecycle or state.","How state works with portals\\nA portal in React lets you render child elements in another part of the DOM tree, outside the parent component. That does not affect the component's state, however. The state stays bound to React's logical tree rather than to the physical DOM.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nIn this example the Modal component is rendered in #modal-root, but its count state is managed just as if it were inside the parent. React's events, lifecycle and context keep working as usual.\\n\\nConclusion\\nPortals are useful for modal windows, tooltips and other elements that have to be outside the document's flow while still preserving the full integration with React's state and context.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\nimport ReactDOM from 'react-dom';\\n\\nfunction Modal({ children }) {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>+</button>\\n    </div>,\\n    document.getElementById('modal-root')\\n  );\\n}"],[2640,"A ready-made library (Material UI, for example) speeds the development up and provides ready-made components and accessibility, but it can limit the customization and increase the bundle size. A custom UI kit gives complete control over the design and the performance, but it requires more time for the development and the maintenance. The choice depends on the project's requirements: speed vs uniqueness.","Introduction\\nThe choice between a ready-made UI library and a custom UI kit is one of the key architectural decisions when creating an interface. Ready-made libraries such as Material UI, Ant Design or Chakra UI offer a set of pre-built components with a single style. A custom UI kit is developed from scratch for the particular needs of the project.\\n\\nThe pros and cons of a ready-made library\\n\\n- The pros: a fast start, built-in accessibility (a11y), documentation, a community, fewer bugs.\\n- The cons: limited customization, superfluous code (an increase in the bundle), a dependency on the updates, the difficulty of integration with a unique design.\\n\\nThe pros and cons of a custom UI kit\\n\\n- The pros: complete control over the styles and the behaviour, a minimal bundle size, an exact correspondence to the design system, independence from external updates.\\n- The cons: a long development, the need to support the accessibility, the testing and the documentation, the risk of bugs.\\n\\nAn example of the code: a custom button component\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a ready-made library for fast prototypes or for projects with a standard design. A custom UI kit is justified for large products with unique branding and strict performance requirements.","// A custom button with props for the styles\\nconst Button = ({ variant, children }) => {\\n  const baseStyle = { padding: '8px 16px', border: 'none', cursor: 'pointer' };\\n  const variants = {\\n    primary: { backgroundColor: 'blue', color: 'white' },\\n    secondary: { backgroundColor: 'gray', color: 'black' },\\n  };\\n  return <button style={{ ...baseStyle, ...variants[variant] }}>{children}</button>;\\n};"],[2641,"The choice depends on the component's complexity and on the project's requirements. For the standard solutions (a date picker, a select) I normally use ready-made libraries, since they save time and have been tested by the community. For unique or heavily customized components (complex tables) I write them myself, in order to ensure the flexibility and the performance.","The approach to the choice between ready-made libraries and custom development\\nWhen implementing complex UI components such as a date picker, tables with sorting and filtering, or custom selects, it matters to assess several factors: the requirements on the functionality, the need for customization, the performance and the development time. Ready-made libraries (React Select, Ant Design, Material-UI, for example) provide proven solutions with broad functionality, which speeds the development up and reduces the number of bugs. They can be excessive in size, however, or may not match the specific design requirements.\\n\\nWhen to use ready-made libraries\\n\\n- Standard components with typical behaviour (a date picker, a select with a search).\\n- A need for fast prototyping or an MVP.\\n- The absence of strict requirements on the customization of the appearance.\\n\\nWhen to write them yourself\\n\\n- Unique requirements on the behaviour or the appearance, which the libraries do not cover.\\n- A need to optimize the performance for large data (the virtualization of tables, for example).\\n- A wish to minimize the bundle size and the dependencies.\\n\\nAn example: a custom select with virtualization\\n\\nIn this example the custom select uses virtualization for working with thousands of options, which cannot be implemented efficiently by means of the standard libraries without additional configuration.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe optimal approach is to combine ready-made libraries for the typical tasks with custom development for the unique requirements, which lets you balance between the speed of the development and the flexibility.","import React, { useState, useRef } from 'react';\\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst VirtualSelect = ({ options }) => {\\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\\n  const [selected, setSelected] = useState(null);\\n  const listRef = useRef();\\n\\n  const handleSelect = (option) => {\\n    setSelected(option);\\n    setIsOpen(false);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setIsOpen(!isOpen)}>\\n        {selected ? selected.label : 'Select...'}\\n      </button>\\n      {isOpen && (\\n        <List\\n          height={200}\\n          itemCount={options.length}\\n          itemSize={35}\\n          ref={listRef}\\n        >\\n          {({ index, style }) => (\\n            <div style={style} onClick={() => handleSelect(options[index])}>\\n              {options[index].label}\\n            </div>\\n          )}\\n        </List>\\n      )}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[2642,"A functional update of the state (the passing of a function into setState with a prev argument) guarantees the new state is computed on the basis of the most current previous state. That is critically important on multiple consecutive setState calls or when working with asynchronous code, since React can batch the updates. The use of prevState prevents errors related to stale values in closures.","The problem of setState's asynchrony\\nIn React the setState method is asynchronous. That means the state is not updated instantly after setState is called. React can group several setState calls into one update, in order to improve the performance (batching). If you use an ordinary assignment — \`setCount(count + 1)\`, for example — then the value of \`count\` is taken from the current closure, which may be stale by the moment of the actual update.\\n\\nHow a functional update works\\nThe functional form \`setCount(prev => prev + 1)\` passes a function into setState, which receives the previous state (prev) as its argument. React guarantees that prev will be the most current value at the moment the update is performed, even if there have been several consecutive calls.\\n\\nAn example with multiple calls\\n\\nWhen does this matter especially\\n\\n- On multiple consecutive updates of the state in one event handler.\\n- When working with asynchronous operations (setTimeout, fetch), where a closure can capture a stale value.\\n- When using the useState hook in functional components, where this problem is also relevant.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA functional update of the state is a reliable way of guaranteeing the correctness of computations that depend on the previous state. It should be used whenever the new state is computed on the basis of the previous one, especially in scenarios with multiple updates or with asynchronous code.","// Wrong: it can lead to the wrong result\\nconst handleClick = () => {\\n  setCount(count + 1); // count = 0\\n  setCount(count + 1); // count = 0 (still 0, from the closure)\\n  // The outcome: count = 1, while 2 was expected\\n};\\n\\n// Right: every call uses the current value\\nconst handleClick = () => {\\n  setCount(prev => prev + 1); // prev = 0 -> 1\\n  setCount(prev => prev + 1); // prev = 1 -> 2\\n  // The outcome: count = 2\\n};"],[2643,"State managers (Redux, for example) are needed for the centralized management of an application's complex state, especially when the data is used by many components. The Context API solves a similar task, but it is less performant with frequent updates, since it causes a re-render of all of the consumers. State managers offer a more predictable update plus tools for debugging.","What are state managers for?\\nIn React the state is normally local to a component. As an application grows, a need arises to share the state between many components, often situated far from one another in the tree. State managers such as Redux, MobX or Zustand provide a single store plus strict rules for changing it, which makes the data flow predictable and makes debugging easier.\\n\\nHow does Context differ?\\nThe Context API is React's built-in mechanism for passing data without prop drilling. It is simple to use, but it has drawbacks: when the context's value changes, every component that consumes it re-renders, even if it does not use the part that has changed. That can lead to performance problems in large applications.\\n\\nAn example of a comparison\\nLet us look at an application with a shopping cart. Using Context:\\n\\nAny component that uses \`useContext(CartContext)\` will be re-rendered on every addition of a product, even if it shows only the total sum.\\n\\nWith Redux:\\n\\nHere \`TotalPrice\` will re-render only if the computed sum changes rather than on any change of the cart.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse Context for simple global state (the theme, the language), which changes rarely. For complex, frequently updated state with a great many consumers, choose a state manager — that will give better performance and predictability.","const CartContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction CartProvider({ children }) {\\n  const [items, setItems] = useState([]);\\n  const addItem = (item) => setItems(prev => [...prev, item]);\\n  return (\\n    <CartContext.Provider value={{ items, addItem }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </CartContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// slice\\nconst cartSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'cart',\\n  initialState: { items: [] },\\n  reducers: {\\n    addItem: (state, action) => { state.items.push(action.payload); },\\n  },\\n});\\n\\n// component\\nfunction TotalPrice() {\\n  const total = useSelector(state =>\\n    state.cart.items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.price, 0)\\n  );\\n  return <div>Total: {total}</div>;\\n}"],[2644,"So that the value is updated only once, on the mounting, you have to pass an empty array of dependencies into the useEffect. That guarantees the effect will run only after the first render. If there is a timer inside the effect, it should be cleared in the function returned, in order to avoid memory leaks.","The problem of the effect running repeatedly\\nIn React components can re-render for various reasons: a change of state, of props or of context. If no dependencies are stated inside a useEffect, the effect will run after every render, which can lead to the undesirable repeated starting of timers or requests.\\n\\nThe solution: an empty array of dependencies\\nBy passing an empty array [] as the second argument of the useEffect, we tell React that the effect depends on no values and should run only once — after the component mounts. That is ideal for the initialization of data, for subscribing to events or for starting a one-off timer.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe importance of cleaning the effects up\\nThe function returned from a useEffect is called when the component unmounts or before the effect runs again. In the example above we clear the interval, in order to avoid memory leaks and attempts to update the state of an unmounted component.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse an empty array of dependencies in a useEffect for running code only on the mounting. That is the standard pattern for the initialization of data, for subscriptions and for one-off operations in React components.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction TimerComponent() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    // This code will run only once, on the mounting\\n    const interval = setInterval(() => {\\n      setValue(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // The clean-up of the timer on the unmounting\\n    return () => clearInterval(interval);\\n  }, []); // an empty array of dependencies\\n\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n}"],[2645,"The clean-up function in a useEffect runs when the component unmounts or before the effect is started again. Without it setInterval will keep working after you leave the component, causing a memory leak and running the callbacks on a component that has already unmounted. That can lead to errors and to uncontrolled behaviour.","The purpose of the clean-up function in useEffect\\nThe cleanup function is a function you return from a useEffect's callback. React calls it when the component unmounts, or before the effect is started again when the dependencies change. That lets you cancel subscriptions, timers, requests or other side effects, in order to avoid memory leaks and incorrect behaviour.\\n\\nWhat will happen without it when setInterval is used?\\nIf you use setInterval inside a useEffect without returning a clean-up function, the interval will keep running even after the component has been removed from the DOM. That leads to:\\n- A memory leak — the interval will never stop.\\n- The running of the callbacks on an unmounted component, which can cause errors (an attempt to update the state of an unmounted component, for example).\\n- The uncontrolled growth of the number of intervals on every re-render, if the dependencies change.\\n\\nAn example with the clean-up\\n\\nIn this example the interval is created once (an empty array of dependencies) and is cleared when the component unmounts. Without the return with the clearInterval, the interval would work for ever, even if the component were hidden.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe clean-up function in a useEffect is obligatory for any long-lived side effects, such as timers, subscriptions or event listeners. It prevents memory leaks and guarantees the proper management of the resources in React applications.","import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Timer() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const id = setInterval(() => {\\n      setCount(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n\\n    // The clean-up function\\n    return () => {\\n      clearInterval(id);\\n    };\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  return <div>{count}</div>;\\n}"],[2646,"The functional form of setState guarantees the update of the state is performed on the basis of the most current previous value. React can group several setState calls into one batch, in order to optimize the performance. If you pass the new value in directly, then on several consecutive setState calls in one event handler, every following call will use a stale state, which will lead to the wrong result. A function, by contrast, receives the previous state as its argument, ensuring the changes accumulate properly.","The problem of setState's asynchrony\\nIn React the setState method is asynchronous. That means the state is not updated instantly after setState is called. React can group several setState calls into one batch, in order to improve the performance, especially in event handlers and in components' lifecycles.\\n\\nWhy the functional form solves the problem\\nWhen you pass an object (a new value) into setState, React simply merges it with the current state. If you have several consecutive setState calls, each of them will use one and the same original state rather than the result of the previous update. The functional form of setState takes a function, which receives the previous state (prevState) and returns the new one. That guarantees every update is based on the freshest state.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen does this matter especially\\n\\n- When performing several updates of the state in one event handler.\\n- When working with asynchronous operations (inside a setTimeout or a fetch, for example), where the batching may not apply.\\n- In functional components with the useState hook, where the functional form is also supported.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse the functional form of setState whenever the new state depends on the previous one. That guarantees the correctness of the updates, especially when the calls are grouped, and makes the code more predictable and more resistant to bugs.","// Wrong: on several calls the counter will increase by only 1\\nhandleClick = () => {\\n  this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\\n  this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\\n  // The result: count will increase by 1 rather than by 2\\n}\\n\\n// Right: the functional form\\nhandleClick = () => {\\n  this.setState(prevState => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));\\n  this.setState(prevState => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }));\\n  // The result: count will increase by 2\\n}"],[2647,"No, wrapping the whole application in React.memo makes no sense and can even make the performance worse. React.memo is intended for preventing redundant re-renders of components that receive the same props. If you wrap the whole application, then on any change of state anywhere a full re-render will happen, since the root component almost always receives new props or state.","What React.memo is, and how it works\\nReact.memo is a higher-order function, which memoizes the result of a component's rendering. It compares the previous and the new props (shallowly) and, if they have not changed, returns the cached result, preventing a re-render. That is useful for components that often receive the same props and render heavy subtrees.\\n\\nWhy the whole application cannot be wrapped\\nAn application's root component normally contains state (through useState, useReducer or a global state manager, for example). On every change of the state the root component receives new props or updates itself, so React.memo will not be able to prevent its re-render. What is more, the comparison of the props at the top level adds redundant computation to no benefit. An example:\\n\\nWhere and how to apply React.memo\\nReact.memo is worth using for components that:\\n- Render often but receive stable props (simple presentational components, for example).\\n- Contain heavy computations or large subtrees.\\n- Do not depend on context or on global state that changes often.\\n\\nAn example of the right usage:\\n\\nIn that case ListItem will not re-render if its particular item has not changed.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact.memo is a tool for targeted optimization rather than a universal solution. Wrapping the whole application is inefficient and can lead to the opposite effect. Use it only for components where it really does bring a benefit, and always profile the performance before and after introducing it.","const App = () => {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Click</button>\\n      <ExpensiveChild />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};\\n\\nexport default React.memo(App); // Useless, since App re-renders when count changes\\n\\nconst ListItem = React.memo(({ item }) => {\\n  return <li>{item.name}</li>;\\n});\\n\\nconst List = ({ items }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {items.map(item => <ListItem key={item.id} item={item} />)}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n};"],[2648,"For a UI library a component-based architecture with atomic design suits best. The components are divided into atoms, molecules and organisms, which ensures the reuse and the flexibility. Every component should be independent, should take props and should not manage global state. That makes testing and maintenance easier.","A component-based architecture for a UI library\\nWhen creating a UI library, it matters to choose an architecture that will ensure the reuse, the scalability and the ease of maintenance. The most suitable approach is a component-based architecture, based on the principles of atomic design. It breaks the interface into small, independent building blocks.\\n\\nThe main principles\\n\\n- Atomic design: the components are divided into atoms (buttons, inputs), molecules (groups of atoms — an input field with a label, for example) and organisms (complex blocks — a form, for example).\\n- Composition: the components should be composable, so that they can be combined for creating complex interfaces.\\n- Independence: every component should not depend on global state or on context; all of the data is passed in through props.\\n- Typing: the use of TypeScript for the strict typing of the props improves the documentation and prevents bugs.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nSuch an architecture is used in the popular libraries, such as Material-UI, Ant Design and Chakra UI. It lets developers customize the components easily and create uniform interfaces.\\n\\nConclusion: a component-based architecture with atomic design is the best choice for a UI library, since it ensures the reuse, the flexibility and the ease of testing. Apply it when creating design systems or corporate libraries of components.","// An atom: a button\\ninterface ButtonProps {\\n  label: string;\\n  onClick: () => void;\\n  variant: 'primary' | 'secondary';\\n}\\n\\nconst Button: React.FC<ButtonProps> = ({ label, onClick, variant }) => {\\n  return <button className={\`btn btn-\${variant}\`} onClick={onClick}>{label}</button>;\\n};\\n\\n// A molecule: an input field with a label\\ninterface InputFieldProps {\\n  label: string;\\n  value: string;\\n  onChange: (value: string) => void;\\n}\\n\\nconst InputField: React.FC<InputFieldProps> = ({ label, value, onChange }) => {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <label>{label}</label>\\n      <input value={value} onChange={(e) => onChange(e.target.value)} />\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n};"],[2649,"Yes, contexts can be nested inside one another. Every Provider creates its own scope, and the child components can obtain the values from the nearest parent Provider of the same type. Nested contexts of different types work independently.","The nesting of contexts in React\\nIn React contexts can be nested one inside another. That means you can have several Provider components wrapped around one another, each of which provides its own value. The child components can obtain access to any of those contexts, using the corresponding useContext hook or Consumer.\\n\\nHow this works\\nWhen a component calls useContext(MyContext), React looks for the nearest Provider of that context in the component tree. If no Provider is found, the default value passed into createContext is used. Nested contexts of different types do not affect one another, but if you nest Providers of one and the same context, the inner Provider overrides the value for its own child elements.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe practical application\\nNested contexts are useful for separating the different aspects of an application's state, such as the theme, the authentication, the localization or the settings. That lets you avoid prop drilling and makes the code cleaner. It is worth remembering, however, that when a context's value changes all of the consumers re-render, so for data that changes often it is better to use other state-management tools.\\n\\nConclusion: the nesting of contexts is a powerful pattern for organizing global state in React, which should be applied for stable or rarely changing data, in order to avoid excessive re-renders.","import React, { createContext, useContext } from 'react';\\n\\nconst ThemeContext = createContext('light');\\nconst UserContext = createContext({ name: 'Guest' });\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value=\\"dark\\">\\n      <UserContext.Provider value={{ name: 'Alice' }}>\\n        <MainContent />\\n      </UserContext.Provider>\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction MainContent() {\\n  const theme = useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  const user = useContext(UserContext);\\n  return <p>{user.name} is using the {theme} theme</p>;\\n}"],[2650,"Context in React lets you pass data (the theme, the language, the user, for example) through the whole component tree without an explicit passing of props at every level. That solves the problem of prop drilling, where data has to be forwarded through many intermediate components. A context is created through createContext, provided through a Provider and consumed through useContext or a Consumer.","What Context is, and what it is for\\nIn React data is normally passed from the top down, through props. As an application grows, a situation arises in which a deeply nested component needs data that sits at the root. Forwarding it through every intermediate component is inconvenient and clutters the code — that is called prop drilling. Context solves that problem, by letting you declare the data once and make it available to any component in the subtree.\\n\\nHow Context works\\nContext consists of three parts: \`createContext\` creates the context object, a \`Provider\` wraps the tree and passes the value in, and \`useContext\` (or a \`Consumer\`) lets the components read that value. When the value in the Provider changes, all of the consumers repaint automatically.\\n\\nHere \`Button\` obtains the theme directly, bypassing \`Toolbar\`. That makes the maintenance easier and the code cleaner.\\n\\nWhen to use Context\\nContext is ideal for global data that changes rarely: the design theme, the interface's language, the authentication data, the user's settings. For data that changes often (a form's state, for example) it is better to use local state or Redux, since an update of the Context causes a repaint of all of the consumers, which can be expensive.\\n\\nConclusion\\nContext is a powerful tool for passing metadata through the component tree without props. Apply it for global settings and for data that many components need, but avoid it for high-frequency updates, so as not to reduce the performance.","const ThemeContext = React.createContext('light');\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <ThemeContext.Provider value=\\"dark\\">\\n      <Toolbar />\\n    </ThemeContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Toolbar() {\\n  return <Button />;\\n}\\n\\nfunction Button() {\\n  const theme = React.useContext(ThemeContext);\\n  return <button className={theme}>A button</button>;\\n}"],[2651,"useMemo is needed for caching the result of a computation between renders. It is worth using when the computation is expensive (a complex sorting or filtering of a large array, for example) and depends on props or state that do not change often. If the computation is simple or the dependencies change every render, useMemo will only add redundant work.","What useMemo is\\nuseMemo is a React hook, which remembers the result of running a function and returns it on the repeat renders, if the dependencies have not changed. That helps you avoid repeat expensive computations and stabilize the references to objects, which is useful for optimizing child components.\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\nUse useMemo when:\\n- The computation takes a noticeable amount of time (the processing of large arrays, complex mathematical operations, for example).\\n- The result is used in the dependencies of other hooks (a useEffect, for example) or is passed into memoized child components (React.memo).\\n- The dependencies change rarely and you want to preserve a stable reference to an object.\\n\\nDo not use useMemo for simple operations (the concatenation of strings, the reading of a property) — that will add the overhead of comparing the dependencies and holding the cache.\\n\\nAn example\\n\\nHere the filtering is performed only when items or filter changes rather than on every render.\\n\\nConclusion\\nuseMemo is a tool for targeted optimization. Apply it deliberately, when there is a real performance problem or a need for stable references. In every other case, prefer simple code without memoization.","import { useMemo, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction List({ items, filter }) {\\n  const filtered = useMemo(() => {\\n    return items.filter(item => item.name.includes(filter));\\n  }, [items, filter]);\\n\\n  return <ul>{filtered.map(item => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>;\\n}"],[2652,"Actions are objects describing the intention to change the state — { type: 'ADD_TODO', payload: 'buy some milk' }, for example. A reducer is a pure function, which takes the current state and an action and returns the new state. A reducer does not change the original state but creates a new one. Together they form a predictable data flow: the component calls an action, Redux passes it into the reducer, and the state updates.","The basics of Redux: actions and reducers\\nIn React, Redux is often used for managing complex state. The key concepts are actions and reducers. Actions are simple objects, which describe what has happened in the application. They contain an obligatory \`type\` field (an identifier string) and an optional \`payload\` (the data). For example, \`{ type: 'INCREMENT', payload: 1 }\`.\\n\\nA reducer is a pure function, which determines how the state changes in response to an action. It takes two arguments: the current state and the action, and returns the new state. It matters that a reducer does not mutate the original state but creates a copy of it with the changes. That makes the state predictable and easy to test.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the action creator returns an action object, which is then passed into the reducer through \`dispatch\`. The reducer handles the action and returns the new state. Such an approach lets you manage the state centrally and track the changes easily.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nRedux is used in large React applications, where many components exchange data. It is especially useful when working with server data, with caching, with complex forms and with collaborative editing. For simple applications, however, you can get by with the built-in \`useState\` or \`useReducer\`.\\n\\nIn summary: actions and reducers are the foundation of Redux. They ensure a predictable update of the state and make debugging and testing easier. Apply Redux when the state becomes complex and requires global access from many components.","// an action creator\\nconst increment = (amount) => ({\\n  type: 'INCREMENT',\\n  payload: amount\\n});\\n\\n// a reducer\\nconst counterReducer = (state = 0, action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'INCREMENT':\\n      return state + action.payload;\\n    case 'DECREMENT':\\n      return state - action.payload;\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// the usage in a store\\nconst store = createStore(counterReducer);\\nstore.dispatch(increment(5));\\nconsole.log(store.getState()); // 5"],[2653,"Keys in React help you identify the elements of a list on an update. They let React match the old and the new elements exactly, minimizing the repaints and avoiding errors with the state. Without keys React may update the elements wrongly, which leads to bugs and to reduced performance.","What are keys in React for?\\nWhen rendering lists, React uses the reconciliation algorithm in order to compare the previous and the new state of the virtual DOM. Keys are special attributes, which help React identify every element of a list uniquely. Without keys React cannot determine exactly which element has been changed, added or deleted, which can lead to the interface being updated incorrectly and to the loss of the components' state.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen you render an array of elements, React expects every element to have a unique key. That key has to be stable and unique among the neighbouring elements. React uses the keys for matching the elements between renders: if a key matches, React considers that it is the same element and updates it; if the key is new, it adds the element; if the key has disappeared, it deletes the element.\\n\\nIn this example the key is the unique identifier \`id\`. If you use the array index as the key, that can cause problems when the order of the elements changes, since React will match the elements wrongly and may lose the state (the text typed in an input, for example).\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Use stable unique identifiers (an ID from a database, for example) as the keys.\\n- Avoid using the array index as the key if the order of the elements can change.\\n- The keys have to be unique only among the neighbouring elements, not necessarily globally.\\n- Do not use random values (Math.random(), for example) as the keys, since they will change on every render, which will lead to a complete repaint of the list.\\n\\nConclusion\\nKeys are an important tool for the optimization and the correctness of the rendering of lists in React. They let React update only the elements that have changed efficiently, preserving the state and improving the performance. Always use stable unique keys, in order to avoid hidden bugs and to ensure the interface behaves predictably.","// An example of a list with keys\\nconst items = [\\n  { id: 1, name: 'Apple' },\\n  { id: 2, name: 'Banana' },\\n  { id: 3, name: 'Cherry' }\\n];\\n\\nfunction FruitList() {\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {items.map(item => (\\n        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2654,"React hooks are functions that let you use state and React's other capabilities in functional components. The main hooks: useState for managing state, useEffect for side effects, useContext for access to a context, useReducer for complex state logic, useMemo and useCallback for optimization. There are also custom hooks, which are created for reusing logic.","What are React hooks?\\nReact hooks appeared in version 16.8 and changed the approach to writing components. Previously state and the lifecycle were available only in class components, but hooks let you use those capabilities in functional components. That made the code simpler and shorter and also improved the reuse of logic.\\n\\nThe main built-in hooks\\n\\n- useState — it adds local state. It returns an array of the current value plus a function for updating it.\\n- useEffect — it performs side effects (requests, subscriptions, timers) after the render. You can manage the dependencies, in order to control when the effect is started.\\n- useContext — it lets you read the value of a context created through React.createContext, without drilling props through every level.\\n- useReducer — an alternative to useState for more complex state logic, based on actions (similar to Redux).\\n- useMemo — it memoizes the result of a computation, in order to avoid repeat expensive operations on every render.\\n- useCallback — it memoizes a function, so that it is not recreated on every render if the dependencies have not changed.\\n- useRef — it creates a mutable object, which is preserved between renders; it is often used for access to DOM elements.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nCustom hooks\\nYou can create your own hooks, in order to extract repeated logic. A hook for working with localStorage or for handling a form, for example. A custom hook is an ordinary function, which uses other hooks inside.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHooks are the standard way of working with state and effects in modern React applications. They make the code simpler, improve the readability and let you reuse logic. A knowledge of the main hooks is obligatory for any React developer, while the ability to create custom hooks is a sign of a good level.","import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Timer() {\\n  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const interval = setInterval(() => {\\n      setSeconds(prev => prev + 1);\\n    }, 1000);\\n    return () => clearInterval(interval); // the clean-up on the unmounting\\n  }, []); // an empty array — the effect runs once\\n\\n  return <div>Seconds elapsed: {seconds}</div>;\\n}"],[2655,"Local state is used for data that only one component or its children need — the text of an input or whether a modal window is open, for example. Global state is applied when the data is needed by many components at different levels of the tree — the user's data or a cart, for example. If the data is used in several unrelated components or is updated often from different places, choose global state. In every other case local state is simpler and more efficient.","The basic idea\\nIn React, state is the data that affects a component's display. Local state is held inside a particular component through \`useState\` or \`useReducer\` and is available only to it and to its child elements, through props. Global state is the data that is available to the whole application or to a large part of it, normally through context or through libraries such as Redux, Zustand or MobX.\\n\\nWhen to use local state\\nLocal state is ideal for isolated data: the value of an input field, whether a dropdown list is open, the current tab. It requires no additional libraries and is easy to read and to test. If the data is needed only by one component and its direct children, do not move it outside — that makes the code simpler and reduces the number of re-renders.\\n\\nWhen to use global state\\nGlobal state is justified when the data is used in several unrelated components — the authorization, the theme settings, the shopping cart, for example. It is also useful if the data is updated often from different places and prop drilling — the passing of props through many levels — has to be avoided. Global state makes the synchronization easier, but it adds complexity and can reduce the performance with frequent updates.\\n\\nThe criteria for the choice\\n\\n- If the data is needed only by one component — local state.\\n- If the data is needed by several components but they are next to one another in the tree — it can be passed through props, but if that becomes cumbersome, global state is better.\\n- If the data has to be preserved between sessions or used in different parts of the application — global state.\\n- If the frequency of the updates is high and the data is not critical to the whole application — local state.\\n\\nConclusion\\nBegin with local state and add global state only when a real need arises. That will keep the code simple and performant. Global state is a powerful tool, but its excessive use complicates the architecture and makes testing harder.","function SearchInput() {\\n  const [query, setQuery] = useState('');\\n  return <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />;\\n}\\n\\n// An example with Context\\nconst UserContext = createContext(null);\\nfunction App() {\\n  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);\\n  return <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>\\n    <Header />\\n    <Profile />\\n  </UserContext.Provider>;\\n}"],[2656,"Redux is not needed when the application's state is simple and local, when there is no complex exchange of data between the components, or when the project is small. In such cases useState, useReducer or the Context API is enough. Redux is justified at a large scale, with complex logic and a need for predictable state.","When Redux is superfluous\\nRedux is a powerful tool, but its use without a need complicates the code and increases the development time. The main signal that Redux is not needed is the simplicity of the state. If the data is used only inside one component or in a few neighbouring ones, it is better to get by with local state through \`useState\` or \`useReducer\`.\\n\\nRedux is also not needed when the project is small or a prototype. Adding Redux requires the configuration of a store, reducers and actions and the connection of a provider, which creates a lot of boilerplate code. For simple applications that is an unjustified cost.\\n\\nThe alternatives to Redux\\n\\n- useState — for a component's local state.\\n- useReducer — for more complex logic inside a component.\\n- The Context API — for passing data through the component tree without props, if the updates are infrequent.\\n- React Query / SWR — for managing server state and caching.\\n\\nAn example: when Redux is not needed\\n\\nHere there is no point in bringing Redux in — the state is isolated and is not used by other components.\\n\\nWhen Redux really is useful\\n\\n- A large application with a great many components sharing common state.\\n- Complex logic for updating the state (a shopping cart with a great many operations, for example).\\n- A need for debugging and time-travel debugging.\\n- Team development, where uniformity and predictability matter.\\n\\nConclusion: Redux is worth applying only when the complexity of the state management exceeds the cost of introducing it. For most small and medium projects React's built-in means are enough, which makes the code simpler and faster to develop.","// A simple application: a login form\\nfunction LoginForm() {\\n  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');\\n  const [password, setPassword] = useState('');\\n  // Local state is enough\\n  return <input value={email} onChange={e => setEmail(e.target.value)} />;\\n}"],[2657,"The UI has to show a waiting indicator or an optimistic update with a note about the status. A button can become inactive with the text \\"Saved, being applied...\\", for example, while the data is displayed with the note \\"awaiting application\\". That helps the user understand that the action has been accepted but the result is not yet final.","The problem of intermediate states\\nIn systems that use event sourcing or asynchronous processing, an event can be saved in the log but not yet processed and applied to the current state. The user, having sent an action, expects an immediate result, but the system can delay the application. If you simply show nothing, the user will decide the action has not worked and will repeat it, creating duplicates.\\n\\nThe strategies of display\\n\\n- An optimistic update — we show the presumed result at once but mark it as \\"awaiting confirmation\\". In a list of tasks, for example, you can add the new task with a semi-transparent background and a clock icon.\\n- A progress indicator — we show a spinner or a progress bar next to the action; on the \\"Save\\" button, for example, we change the text to \\"Saving...\\" and block a repeat press.\\n- A status line or a toast — we print the message \\"Saved, being applied to the system\\" with the ability to cancel or to retry.\\n\\nAn example of the code (React)\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe proper display of the intermediate states is critical to the UX in asynchronous systems. Use optimistic updates with explicit status indicators, in order to avoid confusion and repeat actions. That matters especially in financial, ordering and collaborative applications, where the delay in the application can be considerable.","function SaveButton({ onSave, isPending }) {\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={onSave} disabled={isPending}>\\n      {isPending ? 'Saved, being applied...' : 'Save'}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// The usage with a state hook\\nconst [isPending, setPending] = useState(false);\\nconst handleSave = async () => {\\n  setPending(true);\\n  await saveEvent(); // we save the event\\n  // we do not wait for the application, we show the status\\n  setPending(false); // or we leave it true until the confirmation\\n};"],[2658,"Hydration is the process in which React takes the HTML already rendered on the server and \\"brings it to life\\", by attaching the event handlers and the state. That lets the page be interactive without a complete repaint. In Next.js the hydration happens automatically once the JS bundle has loaded.","What is hydration?\\nHydration is a key mechanism in React and Next.js, which links the server rendering (SSR) to the client rendering. When the server sends the HTML markup, it already contains all of the content but is not interactive. Hydration is the process in which React \\"brings that static markup to life\\", by binding the virtual tree of components, the state and the event handlers to it.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nIn Next.js, on the first request the server generates the HTML and sends it to the client. The browser displays the content instantly, which improves the SEO and the time to the first byte. The JavaScript bundle is then downloaded, and React performs the hydration: it matches the existing DOM with the virtual tree and adds the interactivity. If the markup matches, React does not repaint the page but only attaches the event listeners.\\n\\nWhy does this matter?\\nHydration lets you combine the advantages of SSR (a fast first response, SEO) with those of an SPA (the interactivity, smooth transitions). Without it the page would be static; with it, it is a full application. In Next.js the hydration is automatic, but it matters to avoid discrepancies between the server and the client rendering; otherwise React may issue warnings.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHydration is the bridge between the server and the client rendering. It is necessary for creating fast, interactive applications in Next.js. An understanding of this process helps you avoid errors when working with SSR and optimize the performance.","// An example: a component with useState\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>{count}</button>\\n}\\n\\n// The server renders the HTML: <button>0</button>\\n// After the hydration React adds the onClick and the state"],[2659,"CSR is when the browser receives minimal HTML and builds the page by means of JavaScript. SSR is when the server generates finished HTML and sends it to the client. The main difference: in CSR the content appears after the JS has run; in SSR at once. SSR is better for SEO and for the first paint; CSR for interactive applications.","An introduction to rendering\\nRendering is the process of turning code into a visible page. In web development there are two main approaches: client-side (CSR) and server-side (SSR). They determine where exactly the HTML the user sees is formed.\\n\\nClient-side rendering (CSR)\\nWith CSR the server sends the browser an empty HTML shell plus a link to the JavaScript bundle. The browser downloads the JS, runs it, and only then creates the content. That is the basis of Single Page Applications (SPAs) — applications in React or Vue, for example.\\n\\nCSR's pros: fast navigation after the first load, less load on the server, smooth transitions. Its cons: a long first load, bad indexing by search engines unless prerendering is configured.\\n\\nServer-side rendering (SSR)\\nWith SSR the server runs the JavaScript code, forms the complete HTML and sends it to the browser. The user sees a finished page at once, while the JS is attached for the interactivity. That is used in Next.js, Nuxt.js and the classic server templates.\\n\\nSSR's pros: a fast first paint, good SEO, it works without JavaScript. Its cons: more load on the server, harder caching, a delay on every request.\\n\\nThe main differences\\n\\n- Where the HTML is formed: in the browser (CSR) or on the server (SSR).\\n- The time to the first content: SSR is faster, CSR requires the JS to be downloaded.\\n- SEO: SSR is indexed better, CSR requires additional configuration.\\n- The interactivity: CSR gives a smoother UX after the load.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe choice between CSR and SSR depends on the tasks. For content sites where SEO matters, choose SSR. For complex interactive applications, where the speed of the navigation matters, CSR will suit. The modern frameworks let you combine both approaches — using SSR for the first pages and CSR for the inner ones, for example.","// An example of CSR: the server delivers an empty div, React fills it\\n// index.html\\n<div id=\\"root\\"></div>\\n<script src=\\"bundle.js\\"><\/script>\\n\\n// App.js\\nReactDOM.render(<App />, document.getElementById('root'));\\n\\n// An example of SSR with Express and React\\napp.get('/', (req, res) => {\\n  const html = ReactDOMServer.renderToString(<App />);\\n  res.send(\`<div id=\\"root\\">\${html}</div>\`);\\n});"],[2660,"A hydration mismatch arises when the HTML generated on the server differs from what React expects on the render on the client. That happens because of unstable data, random values, differences in the time zones or browser APIs. The problems include console errors, the loss of interactivity, the resetting of the state and a repeat rendering. To solve it you have to synchronize the data between the server and the client, use stable values and check the environment.","What is a hydration mismatch?\\nWith server-side rendering (SSR) React generates the HTML on the server and sends it to the client. On the client React then \\"brings that HTML to life\\", by attaching the event handlers and creating the virtual tree. If the markup created on the server does not match what React expects on the first render on the client, a hydration error arises — a hydration mismatch.\\n\\nThe causes\\nThe main causes are the use of unstable data, which can differ between the server and the client. A call of \`Date.now()\` or \`Math.random()\` will give different values, for example. Differences in the time zones or the locale, or the use of browser APIs that are unavailable on the server (\`window\` or \`document\`, for example), also lead to discrepancies. Another frequent cause is conditional rendering that depends on state which is initialized differently on the server and on the client.\\n\\nThe problems related to hydration\\n\\n- Errors in the browser's console, warning of the mismatch.\\n- The loss of interactivity: React may not attach the event handlers to the right elements.\\n- The resetting of the state: if the component repaints, the user's input can be lost.\\n- A repeat rendering: React may repaint the tree completely, which reduces the performance.\\n\\nAn example of the code with the problem\\n\\nHow to avoid it\\nUse stable data, which is the same on the server and on the client. For random or time values, apply a deferred render after the hydration — by means of a \`useEffect\`, for example. Check the environment through \`typeof window !== 'undefined'\` and avoid direct access to the browser APIs during the render. You can also use libraries for synchronizing the state, such as Redux with a server-side store.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA hydration mismatch is a widespread problem in SSR applications, which it matters to understand in order to create reliable, fast interfaces. Apply stable data and deferred initialization, in order to avoid errors and to ensure a smooth hydration.","function App() {\\n  const [time, setTime] = React.useState(new Date().toISOString());\\n  return <div>{time}</div>;\\n}\\n// The server: the time = 2025-01-01T00:00:00Z\\n// The client: the time = 2025-01-01T00:00:01Z (a difference of seconds)\\n// A mismatch arises"],[2661,"So that a table with heavy formulas does not hang, you have to break the computation into parts, use virtualization for drawing only the visible rows, cache the formulas' results, and perform the complex computations in a background thread (a Web Worker). It is also worth applying memoization and deferred computation, in order to recompute only the cells that have changed.","The performance problem of tables\\nWhen a table contains thousands of rows and complex formulas, every operation of input or scrolling can cause a recomputation of all of the dependent cells. That leads to the browser's main thread being blocked, because of which the interface \\"hangs\\". The main task is to minimize the volume of work performed synchronously in the UI thread.\\n\\nThe key approaches\\n\\n- Virtualization: render only the visible rows and columns, using libraries such as react-virtualized or tanstack-virtual. That reduces the number of DOM nodes and speeds the scrolling up.\\n- Memoization: cache the formulas' results for every cell, so that on a repeat computation the saved value is used if the input data has not changed.\\n- Deferred computation: recompute the formulas only for the cells that have changed and for their dependencies rather than for the whole table.\\n- Web Workers: perform the heavy computations in a separate thread, so as not to block the UI. The main thread receives the result asynchronously.\\n\\nAn example of an implementation with memoization\\n\\nAn example with a Web Worker\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor a table with heavy formulas to work smoothly, combine virtualization, memoization and background computation. That matters especially for financial applications, data editors and analytical dashboards, where the user expects an instant reaction to their input.","const cache = new Map();\\nfunction computeCell(row, col, data) {\\n  const key = \`\${row}-\${col}\`;\\n  if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);\\n  // A heavy formula — the sum of a range, for example\\n  const result = data.slice(0, row).reduce((sum, r) => sum + r[col], 0);\\n  cache.set(key, result);\\n  return result;\\n}\\n\\n// worker.js\\nself.onmessage = (e) => {\\n  const { rows, formula } = e.data;\\n  const result = rows.map(row => formula(row));\\n  self.postMessage(result);\\n};\\n\\n// main.js\\nconst worker = new Worker('worker.js');\\nworker.postMessage({ rows: data, formula: (r) => r.a + r.b });\\nworker.onmessage = (e) => updateTable(e.data);"],[2662,"A ready-made design system speeds the development up, ensures the consistency and reduces the cost of maintenance, but it limits the flexibility and may not match a product's unique requirements. Your own components give complete control over the design and the behaviour, but they require more time and resources for the development and the maintenance. The choice depends on the deadlines, the budget, the product's particulars and the team's long-term aims.","Introduction\\nThe choice between a ready-made design system and the development of your own components is a strategic decision, which affects the speed of the development, the product's quality and the cost of maintenance. Ready-made solutions such as Material UI, Ant Design or Bootstrap offer proven components and styles, while your own components let you create a unique user interface that matches the brand and the requirements exactly.\\n\\nThe advantages of ready-made design systems\\nThe use of a ready-made design system provides a fast start: the components are already implemented, tested and documented. That reduces the development time and lets the team concentrate on the business logic. Besides, such systems guarantee the consistency of the interface, since all of the components follow the same rules. Material UI, for example, provides ready-made buttons, forms, modal windows and theming, which makes the creation of responsive interfaces easier.\\n\\nThe limitations of ready-made solutions\\nThe main drawback is the limited flexibility. If the product requires a non-standard design or specific behaviour, you have to override the styles and extend the components, which can lead to conflicts and to the code becoming more complicated. Ready-made systems are also often large in size, which affects the performance unless tree shaking is used.\\n\\nThe advantages of your own components\\nThe development of your own components gives complete control over the design, the accessibility and the performance. That is ideal for products with a unique brand or with complex requirements. You can create a component that fits a specific user scenario perfectly, for example, and optimize it for the particular needs.\\n\\nThe disadvantages of your own components\\nThat requires considerable time and resources: every component has to be designed, implemented, tested and maintained. The risk of failing to meet the accessibility standards and of cross-browser incompatibility also grows, if the team does not have enough experience.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of a ready-made design system is justified for projects with limited deadlines and budgets, where the speed and the consistency matter. Your own components are worth developing when the product requires a unique design or a high degree of customization and the team is ready to invest in the long-term maintenance. Often the optimal solution is a hybrid approach: use a ready-made system as the basis and extend it with your own components for the specific tasks.","// An example of the usage of Material UI\\nimport Button from '@mui/material/Button';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return <Button variant=\\"contained\\">Click me</Button>;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of your own button component\\nfunction CustomButton({ children, onClick }) {\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={onClick} style={{ padding: '10px', borderRadius: '8px' }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[2663,"The virtual DOM is a lightweight copy of the real DOM, which React holds in memory. When the state changes, React updates the virtual DOM, compares it with the previous version (diffing) and applies only the minimal changes to the real DOM. That reduces the number of costly operations on the real DOM and speeds the rendering up.","What is the virtual DOM for?\\nDirect changes of the real DOM really are costly, because every time you change an element the browser recomputes the styles, repaints the page and updates the tree. If you make a lot of such changes in a row, that can lead to noticeable delays. The virtual DOM solves that problem, by giving React the ability to optimize the updates.\\n\\nReact holds the virtual DOM as an ordinary JavaScript object, which describes the interface's structure. When a component's state changes, React creates a new virtual DOM and compares it with the previous one. That process is called reconciliation. As a result React determines which elements exactly have changed and updates only those in the real DOM rather than repainting the whole page.\\n\\nHow does this work in practice?\\nImagine you have a list of 1,000 items and want to change only one. Without the virtual DOM you would have to find that element by hand and update it, which is difficult and prone to errors. With React you simply change the state, and React itself computes what has to be updated.\\n\\nHere React compares the old and the new virtual DOM, finds the element that has changed by its key and updates only it in the real DOM. The remaining elements stay untouched.\\n\\nWhen to use the virtual DOM?\\nThe virtual DOM is especially useful in applications with a dynamic interface, where the data changes often: chats, dashboards, forms with validation. It also makes the development easier, since you do not think about how to update the DOM efficiently by hand — React does that for you.\\n\\nIn summary: the virtual DOM is an optimization layer, which lets React minimize the costly operations on the real DOM, making applications faster and the code simpler. It is worth using in any React application, especially when working with large, frequently updated interfaces.","// An example: the update of one element in a list\\nfunction List({ items }) {\\n  const [list, setList] = useState(items);\\n\\n  const updateItem = (id, newText) => {\\n    setList(prev => prev.map(item => \\n      item.id === id ? { ...item, text: newText } : item\\n    ));\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <ul>\\n      {list.map(item => (\\n        <li key={item.id}>{item.text}</li>\\n      ))}\\n    </ul>\\n  );\\n}"],[2664,"A UI kit is a set of reusable interface components, united by a single design and logic. The approaches include the classic libraries with ready-made styles, CSS frameworks, and headless components, which separate the logic from the visual presentation. Headless components provide only the behaviour and the state, while the developer sets the markup and the styles themselves, which gives flexibility and control.","What a UI kit is, and what it is for\\nA UI kit is a collection of ready-made interface elements (buttons, input fields, modal windows and so on), which are used for speeding the development up and maintaining the visual consistency. It can include not only the components but also the design tokens (the colours, the fonts, the spacing), plus documentation on the usage.\\n\\nThe main approaches to developing a UI kit\\n\\n- The classic libraries of components — Material UI or Ant Design, for example. They provide ready-made styles and behaviour, but they often limit the customization.\\n- CSS frameworks — Bootstrap, Tailwind. They give a set of classes and utilities, but they do not always cover complex logic.\\n- Headless components — they separate the logic from the presentation. The library returns only the state and the handlers, while the developer writes the markup and the styles.\\n\\nWhat are headless components\\nA headless component is a function or a hook, which manages the state and the behaviour but renders no markup. A headless hook for a dropdown list, for example, can return \`isOpen\`, \`toggle\` and \`selectedItem\`, while the developer decides how to display the list — through a \`div\`, a \`ul\` or something else.\\n\\nThe advantages of the headless approach\\n\\n- Complete freedom in the markup and the styles — it is easy to adapt to the brand.\\n- The logic is reused between different projects.\\n- Testing becomes easier, since there is no dependency on the DOM.\\n- It is convenient for creating design systems, where the visuals change often.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHeadless components are worth applying when maximum flexibility and control over the appearance is needed, especially in large projects with a unique design. The classic UI kits suit a fast start, but the headless approach is better for long-term maintenance and scaling.","// An example of a headless hook for a modal window\\nfunction useModal() {\\n  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);\\n  const open = () => setIsOpen(true);\\n  const close = () => setIsOpen(false);\\n  return { isOpen, open, close };\\n}\\n\\n// The usage in a component\\nfunction MyModal() {\\n  const { isOpen, open, close } = useModal();\\n  return (\\n    <>\\n      <button onClick={open}>Open</button>\\n      {isOpen && (\\n        <div className=\\"custom-overlay\\">\\n          <div className=\\"custom-content\\">\\n            <p>The content</p>\\n            <button onClick={close}>Close</button>\\n          </div>\\n        </div>\\n      )}\\n    </>\\n  );\\n}"],[2665,"Yes, if the function is declared inside the component and is used in the useEffect's dependencies, it has to be wrapped in useCallback. That prevents the function being recreated on every render, which would otherwise lead to an infinite loop of the effect being called. Without useCallback the function will be new on every render, and the useEffect will fire every time.","The problem with functions in the dependencies\\nIn React, when you use a function in a useEffect's array of dependencies, it matters that that function be stable (that it have the same reference between renders). If the function is declared inside the component, it is created afresh on every render. That means its reference changes, and the useEffect will consider that the dependency has changed, causing the effect to run after every render. That can lead to an infinite loop if the effect changes the state.\\n\\nThe solution with useCallback\\nuseCallback returns a memoized version of the function, which changes only when its own dependencies change. By wrapping the function in useCallback, you guarantee the reference to the function stays stable between renders if its dependencies have not changed. That lets the useEffect determine properly when the effect really does have to be restarted.\\n\\nIn this example handleClick is wrapped in a useCallback with an empty array of dependencies, so it is created once and stays stable. The useEffect will be called only on the first render rather than on every click.\\n\\nWhen useCallback does not have to be used\\nIf the function is not used in a useEffect's dependencies or in other hooks such as useMemo, wrapping it in useCallback is not obligatory. Also, if the function is declared outside the component or is a pure function that does not depend on the props or the state, it can be used directly without memoization.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse useCallback for the functions that are passed into a useEffect's or a useMemo's dependencies, or into child components, in order to avoid unnecessary repeat calls and to optimize the performance. That matters especially in large applications, where redundant renders can slow things down.","import React, { useState, useEffect, useCallback } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Component() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  // The function is stable until count changes\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }, []);\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    console.log('Effect runs');\\n  }, [handleClick]);\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;\\n}"],[2666,"The direct mutation of an array in React's state breaks the principle of immutability, which leads to re-renders being skipped, to unpredictable behaviour of the components and to difficulties in debugging. React compares the references to the state objects, and if the reference has not changed, it considers that the state has not changed. That also breaks the working of React.memo, PureComponent and hooks such as useEffect, which depend on the state's identity.","The problem of the direct mutation of the state\\nIn React the state has to be updated immutably — that is, by the creation of a new object or array rather than by changing the existing one. A direct mutation, through \`push\` or through changing an element by index, for example, leads to the reference to the state object staying the same. React uses a comparison of the references (a shallow comparison) in order to determine the changes, so on a mutation it does not detect the change and does not start a repeat render of the component.\\n\\nThe consequences\\n\\n- The component does not repaint, and the user sees stale data.\\n- The working of \`React.memo\` and \`PureComponent\` is broken, which optimize the render on the basis of a comparison of the props and the state.\\n- Hooks such as a \`useEffect\` with dependencies on the state do not fire, since the reference has not changed.\\n- Debugging and testing become harder, since the state can be changed in several places without control.\\n\\nThe right approach\\nRather than a mutation, you have to create a new array. To add an element, for example, use \`concat\` or the spread operator; to remove one, \`filter\`; to update one, \`map\`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe immutable update of the state is a fundamental principle of React, which ensures the predictability, the performance and the correct working of the optimizations. Always create new objects and arrays when changing the state, in order to avoid hard-to-catch bugs and to ensure the render is correct.","// Wrong (a mutation)\\nconst addItem = (item) => {\\n  state.push(item);\\n  setState(state);\\n};\\n\\n// Right (immutably)\\nconst addItem = (item) => {\\n  setState([...state, item]);\\n};\\n\\n// The update of an element\\nconst updateItem = (id, newValue) => {\\n  setState(state.map(item => item.id === id ? { ...item, ...newValue } : item));\\n};\\n\\n// The removal of an element\\nconst removeItem = (id) => {\\n  setState(state.filter(item => item.id !== id));\\n};"],[2667,"A high-load interface is an interface that preserves its responsiveness and stability with a large number of simultaneous users, intensive interaction and large volumes of data. That is achieved through the optimization of the rendering, the efficient management of the state, caching, the virtualization of lists and the minimization of network requests. It also matters to take the server infrastructure and the load balancing into account.","What is a high-load interface\\nA high-load interface is not merely a beautiful UI but a system capable of handling thousands of requests per second without losing performance. The main aim is to ensure smooth working (60 FPS), a minimal response time and the absence of blocking during the interaction. That is achieved through a comprehensive approach: from the frontend's architecture to the server-side optimization.\\n\\nThe key aspects\\n\\n- The optimization of the rendering: the use of virtualization for lists (react-window, for example), the memoization of the components, the avoidance of redundant repaints.\\n- The management of the state: the efficient use of Redux or the Context API, the separation of the state into global and local, the use of selectors for minimizing the computation.\\n- The network requests: the caching of the responses, debouncing and throttling, the use of WebSockets for real time, pagination and the incremental loading of the data.\\n- The server side: load balancing, horizontal scaling, the use of a CDN for the static files, the optimization of the API (GraphQL, gRPC).\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nThis code renders only the visible rows rather than all 10,000, which reduces the load on the DOM considerably.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe creation of high-load interfaces requires a systematic approach: the optimization of the client side, the efficient management of the data and a scalable server infrastructure. That is necessary for products with mass use, such as social networks, online banks or trading platforms, where even a small delay can lead to the loss of users.","// The virtualization of a list with react-window\\nimport { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';\\n\\nconst Row = ({ index, style }) => (\\n  <div style={style}>Row {index}</div>\\n);\\n\\nconst Example = () => (\\n  <List height={600} itemCount={10000} itemSize={35} width=\\"100%\\">\\n    {Row}\\n  </List>\\n);"],[2668,"React.lazy must be used with the Suspense component. Suspense lets you show a fallback UI (a spinner, for example) while the lazily loaded component is not yet ready. Without Suspense React will issue an error, since it will not be able to handle the state of waiting for the load.","What React.lazy is, and what it is for\\nReact.lazy is a function built into React, which lets you load components only when they really are needed. That is called deferred loading, or code splitting. Rather than including all of the code in one big bundle, you break the application into parts, which are loaded on demand. That reduces the initial size of the download and speeds the application's start up.\\n\\nThe role of Suspense\\nSuspense is a wrapper component, which tells React that there is an asynchronous operation inside it (the loading of a lazy component, for example). While the loading has not completed, Suspense displays the fallback content passed in through the \`fallback\` prop. Without Suspense React does not know what to show during the wait and throws an error. React.lazy is therefore always used in a pair with Suspense.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nIn this example the Dashboard component will be loaded only once App has been rendered. While Dashboard loads, the user will see the text \\"Loading...\\".\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nReact.lazy and Suspense are useful in large applications that have routes or heavy components that are not needed at the start. An admin page, charts or a code editor can be loaded lazily, for example. That improves the performance and reduces the load on the network.\\n\\nIn summary\\nUse React.lazy together with Suspense for the deferred loading of components. That is the standard way of implementing code splitting in React and an obligatory condition for lazy loading to work properly.","import React, { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./Dashboard'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Dashboard />\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n}"],[2669,"React.lazy lets you load components only when they really are needed, reducing the initial bundle size. For that, a dynamic import is used plus a wrapper in Suspense, which shows fallback content during the loading. That improves the application's performance, especially with a large number of routes or with heavy components.","What is React.lazy?\\nReact.lazy is a function built into React, which lets you defer the loading of a component until the moment of its first rendering. Rather than including the whole of the component's code in the application's main bundle, React.lazy uses a dynamic import, which creates a separate chunk. That chunk is loaded asynchronously when the component is first required.\\n\\nThe main aim is to reduce the initial loading time, especially for large applications with a great many routes or rarely used functions. That is part of the code-splitting technique, which breaks the code into parts and loads them as they are needed.\\n\\nHow do you apply React.lazy?\\nTo use React.lazy you have to import the component through a dynamic import and wrap it in the Suspense component. Suspense takes a fallback prop, which is displayed while the lazy component loads. Normally that is a spinner or a placeholder.\\n\\nIn this example HeavyComponent will be loaded only once App renders. If the component is not used at once, its code does not reach the main bundle.\\n\\nWhere to apply it?\\nReact.lazy is especially useful together with React Router, for the lazy loading of the pages. You can load every page separately, for example, so that the user does not download all of the application's code at once.\\n\\nSuch an approach cuts the initial bundle size down and speeds the loading of the first page up.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact.lazy is a simple, effective way of optimizing the performance of React applications. It is worth applying for large components that are not needed on the first render, especially for routes or modal windows. That helps you reduce the loading time and improve the user experience.","import React, { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';\\n\\nconst HeavyComponent = lazy(() => import('./HeavyComponent'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <HeavyComponent />\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { BrowserRouter, Routes, Route } from 'react-router-dom';\\nimport { lazy, Suspense } from 'react';\\n\\nconst Home = lazy(() => import('./pages/Home'));\\nconst About = lazy(() => import('./pages/About'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <BrowserRouter>\\n      <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading page...</div>}>\\n        <Routes>\\n          <Route path=\\"/\\" element={<Home />} />\\n          <Route path=\\"/about\\" element={<About />} />\\n        </Routes>\\n      </Suspense>\\n    </BrowserRouter>\\n  );\\n}"],[2670,"React Hook Form lets you add validation easily, through rules passed into register. You can use the built-in rules (required, minLength, pattern) or custom validate functions. The errors are available in the errors object, and the messages are printed through errors.field.message. For the complex scenarios a resolver with Yup or Zod is applied.","The basics of validation in React Hook Form\\nReact Hook Form is a library for managing forms in React, which minimizes the number of re-renders and makes validation easier. The validation is configured directly in the register method, which binds the field to the form's state. You pass in an object with the rules, and the library checks the values automatically on the submission or on a change.\\n\\nThe built-in rules cover most of the basic cases: required (an obligatory field), minLength/maxLength (the length), pattern (a regular expression), min/max (numeric bounds). For more complex logic the validate function is used, which returns true or a string with an error.\\n\\nAn example of basic validation\\n\\nCustom validation through validate\\nIf you have to check that a password contains a digit, for example, use the validate function. It receives the field's current value and has to return true or a string with an error.\\n\\nThe integration with Yup or Zod\\nFor complex validation schemas it is convenient to use a resolver. Install @hookform/resolvers and choose a Yup or a Zod schema. That lets you move the rules into a separate file and reuse them.\\n\\nConclusion\\nReact Hook Form provides flexible, performant tools for validation. For simple forms the built-in rules are enough; for complex ones use a resolver with Yup or Zod. That makes the code clean and easily maintainable, especially in large projects.","import { useForm } from 'react-hook-form';\\n\\nfunction MyForm() {\\n  const { register, handleSubmit, formState: { errors } } = useForm();\\n\\n  const onSubmit = (data) => console.log(data);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>\\n      <input {...register('email', {\\n        required: 'The email is required',\\n        pattern: {\\n          value: /^[^@\\\\s]+@[^@\\\\s]+\\\\.[^@\\\\s]+$/,\\n          message: 'An incorrect email'\\n        }\\n      })} />\\n      {errors.email && <p>{errors.email.message}</p>}\\n\\n      <input {...register('age', {\\n        min: { value: 18, message: 'The age has to be ≥ 18' }\\n      })} />\\n      {errors.age && <p>{errors.age.message}</p>}\\n\\n      <button type=\\"submit\\">Send</button>\\n    </form>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nregister('password', {\\n  validate: (value) =>\\n    /\\\\d/.test(value) || 'The password has to contain a digit'\\n})\\n\\nimport { yupResolver } from '@hookform/resolvers/yup';\\nimport * as yup from 'yup';\\n\\nconst schema = yup.object({\\n  email: yup.string().email().required(),\\n  age: yup.number().min(18).required()\\n});\\n\\nuseForm({ resolver: yupResolver(schema) });"],[2671,"Yes, the approach changes. For objects with deep nesting a shallow copy (through a spread, for example) copies only the top level, while the nested objects stay shared references. To preserve the original value completely, you have to use a deep copy: JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) or structuredClone(). The choice depends on whether there are functions, dates or other non-serializable data.","The problem of shallow copying\\nWhen an object has nested structures (objects inside objects, arrays of objects), a simple copy through the spread operator or Object.assign creates a new object, but the inner references stay the same. That means a change of a nested property in the copy will change the original too, which often leads to unexpected bugs.\\n\\nDeep copying\\nTo preserve the original value completely, you have to copy all of the nested objects recursively. In JavaScript there are several ways:\\n- The JSON methods: JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj)) — simple, but it loses functions, undefined, symbols and dates (turning them into strings).\\n- structuredClone(): the modern built-in method; it supports most types, including Date, Map and Set, but not functions.\\n- A recursive function: complete control; you can handle any types, but it requires a manual implementation.\\n\\nWhen to use which approach\\nIf the object contains only serializable data (strings, numbers, arrays, simple objects), the JSON method suits. If there are Dates, Maps, Sets or other special types, use structuredClone. For maximum flexibility (if you have to preserve functions, for example), write your own recursive function. In real projects deep copying is often applied when working with the state in Redux, where mutations have to be avoided, or when cloning complex configurations.\\n\\nIn summary: as the nesting grows, be sure to move from shallow copying to deep copying, choosing the method according to the types of the data. That prevents accidental changes of the original objects and makes the code predictable.","const original = { user: { name: 'Alice', address: { city: 'NYC' } } };\\nconst shallowCopy = { ...original };\\nshallowCopy.user.address.city = 'LA';\\nconsole.log(original.user.address.city); // 'LA' — the original has been changed!\\n\\n// structuredClone — the modern way\\nconst deepCopy = structuredClone(original);\\ndeepCopy.user.address.city = 'Boston';\\nconsole.log(original.user.address.city); // 'NYC' — the original is untouched\\n\\n// A recursive function for complete control\\nfunction deepClone(obj) {\\n  if (obj === null || typeof obj !== 'object') return obj;\\n  if (Array.isArray(obj)) return obj.map(deepClone);\\n  const cloned = {};\\n  for (let key in obj) {\\n    cloned[key] = deepClone(obj[key]);\\n  }\\n  return cloned;\\n}"],[2672,"Yes, another module's selector can be called, if it takes the whole state or part of it. It is better organized through the composition of the selectors: export the base selectors from the module and use them in the other modules, passing in the slice of the state needed. That preserves the encapsulation and makes testing easier.","Introduction\\nIn Redux selectors are pure functions, which take the state and return derived data. They help you avoid duplicated logic and make testing easier. The question of calling one module's selector from another arises when designing large applications, where the state is broken into several slices.\\n\\nThe basic concept\\nSelectors can be of two types: global (they take the whole state) and local (they take only their own slice). If a module's selector is written as a local one, it expects only its own sub-object of the state as its input. To use it in another module, you have to pass in the corresponding slice from the global state. That is done through composition: in the other module a new selector is created, which first extracts the slice needed and then calls the local selector.\\n\\nAn example\\nSuppose there is a \`users\` module with a \`selectUserById\` selector, which takes the \`state.users\` slice plus an id:\\n\\nIn the \`posts\` module a post's author has to be obtained. We can import that selector and pass it the slice from the global state:\\n\\nHere \`selectPostAuthor\` uses the selector from the other module, passing it \`state.users\`. That is possible because the \`selectUserById\` selector does not depend on the global state but works with the slice it is given.\\n\\nThe best practices\\n\\n- Export the local selectors from every module, so that they can be reused.\\n- Avoid creating selectors that address the global state directly inside a module — that makes testing and reuse harder.\\n- Use the composition of selectors for creating new selectors on the basis of the existing ones.\\n- For complex dependencies you can use the \`reselect\` library, which supports memoization and makes the composition easier.\\n\\nConclusion\\nCalling another module's selector is normal practice, if the selectors are written as pure functions taking a slice of the state. Organize the modules so that each exports its own local selectors, and combine them in the other modules by passing in the slice needed. That preserves the cleanliness of the architecture and makes maintaining the code easier.","// users/selectors.js\\nexport const selectUserById = (usersState, id) =>\\n  usersState.entities[id];\\n\\n// posts/selectors.js\\nimport { selectUserById } from '../users/selectors';\\n\\nexport const selectPostAuthor = (state, postId) => {\\n  const post = state.posts.entities[postId];\\n  return selectUserById(state.users, post.authorId);\\n};"],[2673,"A slice in Redux Toolkit unites the reducers, the actions and the selectors for one fragment of the state. The best practices include: the separation of the state into logical modules, the use of createSlice for reducing the boilerplate, the avoidance of mutations through Immer, the normalization of the data, the extraction of the selectors into separate functions, and the use of createAsyncThunk for the asynchronous operations.","An introduction to the slice\\nA slice is a key concept of Redux Toolkit, which lets you unite the reducer, the actions and the selectors for a particular part of the state. That makes the development easier, reduces the amount of boilerplate code and makes the code more readable. To use a slice effectively, however, it matters to follow certain practices.\\n\\nThe main best practices\\n\\n- Separate the state into logical modules: every slice should be responsible for one functional area (the users, the cart, the filters, for example). That makes maintenance and testing easier.\\n- Use createSlice: it generates the actions and the reducers automatically, which reduces the duplication of code. Rather than creating the action types and the action creators by hand, for example, you simply describe the reducers.\\n- Avoid mutations directly: Redux Toolkit uses Immer, so you can write mutating code in the reducers, but that does not mean it should be abused. Try to return new objects where possible, in order to preserve the predictability.\\n- Normalize the data: for complex nested structures, use normalization (by means of createEntityAdapter, for example). That makes updating and finding the items easier.\\n- Extract the selectors: define the selectors in the slice's file or separately, in order to reuse them in the components. That improves the performance and the readability.\\n- Use createAsyncThunk for the asynchronous operations: that is the standard way of handling requests to an API, which manages the pending, fulfilled and rejected states automatically.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere to apply it\\nThese practices are useful in any React application that uses Redux, especially when the state becomes complex. They help you maintain the cleanliness of the code and make testing and scaling easier.\\n\\nConclusion\\nBy following these best practices you create a predictable, easily maintainable state. Use a slice for the modularity, createAsyncThunk for the asynchrony, and normalization for complex data — that will make your application more reliable and more convenient to develop.","import { createSlice, createAsyncThunk, createEntityAdapter } from '@reduxjs/toolkit';\\n\\nconst usersAdapter = createEntityAdapter();\\n\\n// An asynchronous thunk for loading the users\\nexport const fetchUsers = createAsyncThunk('users/fetchUsers', async () => {\\n  const response = await fetch('/api/users');\\n  return response.json();\\n});\\n\\nconst usersSlice = createSlice({\\n  name: 'users',\\n  initialState: usersAdapter.getInitialState({ status: 'idle' }),\\n  reducers: {\\n    // The synchronous reducers\\n    userAdded: usersAdapter.addOne,\\n    userRemoved: usersAdapter.removeOne,\\n  },\\n  extraReducers: (builder) => {\\n    builder\\n      .addCase(fetchUsers.pending, (state) => { state.status = 'loading'; })\\n      .addCase(fetchUsers.fulfilled, (state, action) => {\\n        state.status = 'succeeded';\\n        usersAdapter.setAll(state, action.payload);\\n      })\\n      .addCase(fetchUsers.rejected, (state) => { state.status = 'failed'; });\\n  },\\n});\\n\\n// The selectors\\nexport const { selectAll: selectAllUsers, selectById: selectUserById } = usersAdapter.getSelectors((state) => state.users);\\nexport const selectUsersStatus = (state) => state.users.status;\\n\\nexport const { userAdded, userRemoved } = usersSlice.actions;\\nexport default usersSlice.reducer;"],[2674,"To make a useEffect respond to changes of an object's individual fields, you have to pass the fields themselves into the array of dependencies rather than the object as a whole. Instead of the user object, for example, pass user.name and user.age. If the fields are dynamic, you can use useMemo for creating a stable reference to an object with the fields needed.","The problem with objects in the dependencies\\nIn React, when you pass an object into a useEffect's array of dependencies, the effect fires only when the reference to that object changes. If you mutate the object (change one of its fields, for example), the reference stays the same and the effect is not started. That is a frequent cause of bugs, when developers expect a reaction to a change of the fields.\\n\\nThe solution: pass the individual fields\\nThe simplest way is to state the object's particular fields in the dependencies. For example:\\n\\nSuch an approach works if you know which fields matter. If there are many fields or they are dynamic, you can use useMemo for creating an object with the fields needed:\\n\\nHere useMemo returns a new object only when the fields stated change, so the effect will fire properly.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Avoid passing the whole object into the dependencies if a reaction to the individual fields is needed.\\n- Use useMemo for stabilizing the references to objects that depend on several fields.\\n- Remember that a mutation of an object without a change of the reference will not cause the effect — always create a new object through setState.\\n\\nConclusion: for a useEffect to work properly with objects, always state the particular fields in the dependencies, or use useMemo for creating a stable reference. That makes the code predictable and avoids redundant firings of the effect.","const [user, setUser] = useState({ name: 'Alice', age: 25 });\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log('Name changed:', user.name);\\n}, [user.name]); // it responds only to a change of name\\n\\nconst importantFields = useMemo(() => ({ name: user.name, age: user.age }), [user.name, user.age]);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  console.log('Important fields changed:', importantFields);\\n}, [importantFields]);"],[2675,"In Redux the data moves strictly in one direction. A component calls dispatch(action), the action reaches the reducer, which returns a new state. The store saves the state and notifies the subscribed components, which repaint with the new data. That makes the flow predictable and easy to track.","The basic idea\\nRedux is a library for managing state, which uses a unidirectional data flow. That means the data always moves round one circle: component → action → reducer → store → component. Such an approach makes debugging easier and makes the application's behaviour predictable.\\n\\nThe complete cycle\\n\\n- The component calls \`dispatch(action)\` — that is the only way of changing the state.\\n- An action is a simple object with a \`type\` field and, optionally, a \`payload\`.\\n- A reducer is a pure function, which takes the current state and the action and returns the new state.\\n- The store saves the new state and notifies all of the subscribers through \`subscribe\`.\\n- The components connected through \`connect\` or \`useSelector\` receive the updated data and repaint.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nThe unidirectional flow makes tracking the changes easier: you always know where the data came from and how it changed. That is especially useful in large applications, where many components share the state. Redux also supports middleware (Redux Thunk or Saga, for example) for asynchronous actions, but the basic flow stays unchanged.\\n\\nIn summary: Redux is worth applying when an application's state becomes complex and requires predictable management. It is especially useful in large projects with a great many interacting components, where the centralized holding of the data and clear logic for changing it matter.","// An action creator\\nconst addTodo = (text) => ({\\n  type: 'ADD_TODO',\\n  payload: text\\n});\\n\\n// A reducer\\nconst todosReducer = (state = [], action) => {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'ADD_TODO':\\n      return [...state, { text: action.payload, done: false }];\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// A component\\nconst TodoInput = ({ dispatch }) => {\\n  const handleSubmit = (e) => {\\n    e.preventDefault();\\n    dispatch(addTodo(inputValue));\\n  };\\n  return <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>...</form>;\\n};\\n\\n// The connection\\nconst mapDispatchToProps = { addTodo };\\nexport default connect(null, mapDispatchToProps)(TodoInput);"],[2676,"No, the data cannot be mutated in a reducer. Redux requires that the state be updated immutably, by returning a new object. For arrays use the spread operator, map, filter or concat. For objects, the spread operator or Object.assign. That guarantees the predictability and the proper working of React's repaints.","Why immutability is critical in Redux\\nRedux is built on the principle that the state is an immutable object. Every time you want to change the state, you create a new object rather than modifying the old one. That lets Redux track the changes through a comparison of the references, which makes the application predictable and makes debugging easier. If you mutate the state directly, React may not detect the change and the UI will not update.\\n\\nHow to update arrays properly\\nFor arrays, use the methods that return a new array: \`map\`, \`filter\`, \`concat\`, or the spread operator. To add an item, for example:\\n\\nTo remove an item, use \`filter\`:\\n\\nTo update a particular item, \`map\`:\\n\\nHow to update objects properly\\nFor objects, use the spread operator or \`Object.assign\`. For example:\\n\\nIf the object is nested, update every level separately, so as not to lose the other fields.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Never use \`push\`, \`splice\` or the direct assignment of properties.\\n- For complex structures you can use libraries such as Immer, which let you write mutable code but create immutable updates automatically.\\n- Remember that immutability is not only a rule of Redux but also a good practice for any complex state.\\n\\nConclusion: immutability in Redux is a fundamental requirement, which ensures the predictability and the performance. Always return a new state object, using the spread operator or the methods that return new arrays. To make work with deep structures easier, you can apply Immer.","case 'ADD_TODO':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    todos: [...state.todos, action.payload]\\n  };\\n\\ncase 'REMOVE_TODO':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    todos: state.todos.filter(todo => todo.id !== action.payload)\\n  };\\n\\ncase 'TOGGLE_TODO':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    todos: state.todos.map(todo =>\\n      todo.id === action.payload ? { ...todo, completed: !todo.completed } : todo\\n    )\\n  };\\n\\ncase 'UPDATE_USER':\\n  return {\\n    ...state,\\n    user: {\\n      ...state.user,\\n      name: action.payload.name\\n    }\\n  };"],[2677,"A function is passed into useState for the lazy initialization of the state. React calls it only on the component's first render rather than on every update. That is useful when the initial value requires complex computation or a read from localStorage, so as not to perform it repeatedly. Instead of the value being computed on every render, the function runs once.","What is lazy initialization in useState\\nWhen you pass a function in as the initial value in \`useState\`, React calls it only once — on the component's first render. That is called lazy initialization. Unlike the passing of an ordinary value, which is computed on every render (even if the result is not used), a function guarantees the costly operations are performed only once.\\n\\nAn example with an ordinary value\\n\\nHere \`expensiveFunction()\` will be called on every render, even if the state has already been initialized. That can lead to unnecessary computation.\\n\\nAn example with lazy initialization\\n\\nNow the function runs only on the first render. That is especially useful for complex computations, for reading from localStorage or for parsing large data.\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\n\\n- When the initial value requires heavy computation.\\n- When data has to be read from localStorage or sessionStorage.\\n- When the initial value depends on external data that does not change.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a function in \`useState\` for optimizing the performance when the initialization of the state requires considerable resources. That is a simple practice, which helps you avoid redundant computation and makes the code more predictable.","const [state, setState] = useState(expensiveFunction()); // it is called on every render\\n\\nconst [state, setState] = useState(() => expensiveFunction()); // it is called only once"],[2678,"useCallback returns a memoized version of a function, which changes only when the dependencies change. That helps you avoid redundant re-renders of child components wrapped in React.memo and stabilize the references to the functions for the effects. It is used for optimization, but it does not guarantee a speed-up if the dependencies change often.","What useCallback is\\nuseCallback is a React hook, which returns a memoized (cached) version of a function. It takes two arguments: the function itself and an array of dependencies. React remembers the function and returns the very same reference until the dependencies change. That lets you avoid a new function being created on every render, which matters for the performance in some scenarios.\\n\\nWhat it is for\\nIn React, on every render of a component all of the functions declared inside it are created afresh. If such a function is passed into a child component wrapped in React.memo, the child component's memoization will not fire, because the props (the reference to the function) will be new every time. useCallback solves that problem, by preserving a stable reference. It is also useful for effects that depend on a function, so as not to call them again unnecessarily.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nIn this example Child will not re-render when count changes, because handleClick does not change. Without the useCallback, Child would re-render every time, since the reference to the function would be new.\\n\\nWhen to apply it\\nuseCallback is worth using when a function is passed into memoized child components or is used in a useEffect's dependencies. You should not wrap every function, however — that can add redundant computation. The optimization is justified if the component is heavy or renders often.\\n\\nIn summary: useCallback helps you stabilize the references to functions and improve the performance of React applications, but it should be applied deliberately, only where it really does give a gain.","import React, { useCallback, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n    setCount(c => c + 1);\\n  }, []); // empty dependencies — the function is stable\\n\\n  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;\\n}\\n\\nconst Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {\\n  console.log('Child re-rendered');\\n  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;\\n});"],[2679,"Micro-frontends are an architectural approach, which breaks an application into independent parts that are developed and deployed separately but assembled into a single interface. Web Components are a browser standard for creating reusable UI elements with the encapsulation of the styles and the logic. Micro-frontends solve the problem of scaling the team and of independent deployment; Web Components the problem of isolation and of the reuse of code.","Introduction\\nWhen an application grows, teams often face the need to use different technologies or frameworks in one product. Two popular ways of solving that task are micro-frontends and Web Components. They solve different problems, although they are sometimes confused.\\n\\nMicro-frontends\\nMicro-frontends are an architectural pattern in which the frontend is broken into several independent modules (the micro-frontends). Every module can be developed by a separate team, can use its own technology stack and can be deployed independently. The integration happens at the level of the application: either through iframes, or through a JavaScript contract, or through a server-side assembly.\\n\\nAn example: you have an online shop. The catalogue module is written in React, the cart module in Vue, and the payment module in Angular. All of them are assembled into a single interface but work as separate applications.\\n\\nWeb Components\\nWeb Components are a set of browser standards (Custom Elements, Shadow DOM, HTML Templates), which let you create your own HTML tags with encapsulated logic and styles. They do not depend on a framework and can be used in any application written in any stack.\\n\\nAn example: you create a \`<user-card>\` component, which displays a user's card. That component can be used in React, in Vue and in plain JavaScript.\\n\\nThe key differences\\n\\n- The level of the integration: micro-frontends integrate whole applications; Web Components individual elements of the interface.\\n- The independence: micro-frontends let you develop and deploy whole modules independently; Web Components only reusable UI parts.\\n- The technologies: micro-frontends can use different frameworks; Web Components are a browser standard, independent of any framework.\\n- The complexity: micro-frontends require a complex infrastructure (orchestration, shared routing); Web Components are simpler to use.\\n\\nWhen to choose which\\nMicro-frontends suit large products with several teams, where independence of the development and the deployment is needed. Web Components suit the creation of a library of reusable UI elements, which have to work in any environment.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMicro-frontends and Web Components solve different tasks: the first are organizational and architectural, the second technical and at the level of the UI. They can be combined: inside a micro-frontend you can use Web Components for isolating parts of the interface, for example. The choice depends on the project's scale and on the team's needs.","// An example of the integration through a JavaScript contract\\n// The catalogue micro-frontend (React)\\nwindow.microfrontendCatalog = {\\n  mount: (container) => {\\n    ReactDOM.render(<Catalog />, container);\\n  },\\n  unmount: (container) => {\\n    ReactDOM.unmountComponentAtNode(container);\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// The main application calls mount when it is needed\\nwindow.microfrontendCatalog.mount(document.getElementById('catalog-root'));\\n\\n// The definition of a Web Component\\nclass UserCard extends HTMLElement {\\n  connectedCallback() {\\n    this.innerHTML = \`<div class=\\"card\\">\${this.getAttribute('name')}</div>\`;\\n  }\\n}\\ncustomElements.define('user-card', UserCard);\\n\\n// The usage in any application\\n<user-card name=\\"Ivan\\"></user-card>"],[2680,"In class components React's methods are not bound to the class's instance automatically. When a method is passed as an event handler, the this context is lost, so you have to bind the method explicitly through bind, through an arrow function, or by declaring the method as an arrow function, in order to preserve the access to this and to the component's state.","The problem of the loss of the context\\nIn JavaScript a class's methods are not bound to the instance automatically. When you pass a method as a callback (into an event handler, for example), it is called without the context, and this becomes undefined (in strict mode) or the global object. In React that leads to errors when you try to access this.state or this.props.\\n\\nThe ways of solving it\\n\\n- The use of \`bind\` in the constructor: \`this.handleClick = this.handleClick.bind(this)\`.\\n- Arrow functions in the JSX: \`onClick={() => this.handleClick()}\`.\\n- The declaration of the methods as arrow functions (class fields): \`handleClick = () => { ... }\`.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWithout the bind in the constructor, this.increment would have lost the context, and the call of this.setState would have caused an error.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe explicit binding of the methods is necessary for preserving the access to the component's state and props. In modern React it is preferable to use functional components with hooks, where this problem does not exist, but when working with class components it matters to remember the binding.","class Counter extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.state = { count: 0 };\\n    // The explicit binding\\n    this.increment = this.increment.bind(this);\\n  }\\n\\n  increment() {\\n    this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <button onClick={this.increment}>+1</button>;\\n  }\\n}"],[2681,"A stale closure arises when a callback captures a stale value from the closure. To fix that without adding dependencies, you can use useRef for holding the current value and address it inside the callback. That lets you preserve a stable reference to the function while always using fresh data.","What is a stale closure?\\nA stale closure is the situation in which a function created inside a hook (a useCallback, for example) remembers the values of the variables from the render in which it was created. If those variables change on the subsequent renders, the callback keeps using the old values, which leads to bugs.\\n\\nThe problem with the dependency array\\nNormally, to solve that problem the variable is added to the useCallback's array of dependencies. That leads to the function being recreated on every change of the variable, however, which can be undesirable for the performance or for the stability of the references (when passing it into memoized child components, for example).\\n\\nThe solution through useRef\\nUse useRef for holding the current value. Create a ref, update it on every render (through a useEffect, for example), and address ref.current inside the callback. Thus the callback will always see the fresh value while its reference stays stable.\\n\\nWhen is this useful?\\nThis approach is useful when a stable function has to be passed into a memoized component (React.memo) or into a useEffect with empty dependencies while the function still has to use the current data. For event handlers or subscriptions, for example.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe use of useRef is an elegant way of avoiding a stale closure without recreating the function. It suits the cases in which the stability of the reference matters while fresh data is needed. Do not abuse it, however: if there are few dependencies and they change rarely, it is simpler to add them to the array of dependencies.","const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\nconst countRef = useRef(count);\\n\\nuseEffect(() => {\\n  countRef.current = count;\\n}, [count]);\\n\\nconst handleClick = useCallback(() => {\\n  console.log(countRef.current); // always the current value\\n}, []); // empty dependencies"],[2682,"useEffectEvent is an experimental React hook, which lets you call functions inside effects without adding them to the dependencies. It is needed for separating the logic that should not cause the effect to run again from the reactive values. That makes work with effects easier and reduces the number of bugs related to the dependencies.","What is useEffectEvent?\\nuseEffectEvent is a new hook, presented in React 19 (previously known as useEffectEvent in the experimental version). It is intended to solve the problem in which, inside a useEffect, a function has to be called that depends on external values but should not cause the effect to run again when they change. Normally such functions are added to the array of dependencies, which leads to redundant calls of the effect. useEffectEvent lets you move that function out of the dependencies while preserving the access to the current values.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nThe hook returns a stable function, which always has access to the latest values of the props and the state but is not reactive. That means the effect will not restart when those values change, while the function inside the effect will use the fresh data. That is especially useful for event handlers, subscriptions or logging.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nIn this example onMessage is not added to the dependencies, but when it is called it uses the current theme. If we had used an ordinary function, we would have had to add it to the dependencies, which would have caused a reconnection on every change of theme.\\n\\nWhen to apply it?\\nuseEffectEvent is worth using when, inside an effect, a function is called that depends on external values but should not affect the restarting of the effect. That improves the readability and the performance, by avoiding redundant calls. The hook is still experimental, however, so in production it is worth waiting for a stable version or using alternative approaches — a useRef for holding the current values, for example.\\n\\nIn summary: useEffectEvent is a powerful tool for managing effects in React, which helps you separate the reactive logic from the non-reactive, making the code cleaner and more predictable.","import { useEffect, useEffectEvent } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Chat({ roomId, theme }) {\\n  const onMessage = useEffectEvent((msg) => {\\n    console.log(theme, msg); // always the current theme\\n  });\\n\\n  useEffect(() => {\\n    const connection = createConnection(roomId);\\n    connection.on('message', onMessage);\\n    return () => connection.disconnect();\\n  }, [roomId]); // onMessage is not in the dependencies\\n}"],[2683,"Code splitting lets you break one large application into several parts, which are loaded as they are needed. In React that is done by means of dynamic imports and React.lazy. The main principle is to split by route or by large component, so that the user receives only the code needed. That speeds the initial loading up and saves traffic.","What code splitting is, and what it is for\\nWhen a React application is built into one chunk, all of the code — the libraries, the components, the utilities — ends up in one file. On the first visit to the site, the browser downloads that file in its entirety, even if the user sees only the login page. That slows the loading down and increases the consumption of traffic. Code splitting solves that problem: the application is broken into several parts, which are loaded only when they really are needed.\\n\\nHow this works in React\\nThe main tool is the dynamic import. Rather than the static \`import Component from './Component'\`, \`const Component = React.lazy(() => import('./Component'))\` is used. React.lazy lets you defer the loading of a component until the moment of its rendering. To handle the loading state, the \`Suspense\` component is used, which shows a placeholder while the chunk loads.\\n\\nThe principles of the splitting\\nThe code has to be split along the application's logical boundaries. The most widespread approach is splitting by route: every page becomes a separate chunk. You can also extract heavy libraries (charts or editors, for example) into separate chunks, so that they do not slow the initial loading down. It matters not to fragment the code too finely — every chunk has the overhead of an HTTP request, so it is optimal for a chunk's size to be no smaller than 10–20 KB.\\n\\nThe configuration in Webpack\\nWebpack creates separate chunks for the dynamic imports automatically. Additionally you can configure \`splitChunks\`, in order to extract the shared dependencies (React, for example) into a separate vendor chunk. That lets the browser cache the unchanging libraries separately from the application's code.\\n\\nConclusion\\nCode splitting is standard practice for large React applications. It speeds the first load up, reduces the load on the network and improves the user experience. Apply it whenever the application contains several routes or heavy components that are not needed at the start.","import React, { Suspense, lazy } from 'react';\\nconst Dashboard = lazy(() => import('./Dashboard'));\\nconst Settings = lazy(() => import('./Settings'));\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading...</div>}>\\n      <Dashboard />\\n      <Settings />\\n    </Suspense>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\n// webpack.config.js\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  optimization: {\\n    splitChunks: {\\n      chunks: 'all',\\n      cacheGroups: {\\n        vendor: {\\n          test: /[\\\\\\\\/]node_modules[\\\\\\\\/]/,\\n          name: 'vendors',\\n          chunks: 'all',\\n        },\\n      },\\n    },\\n  },\\n};"],[2684,"Localization (i18n) is the process of adapting an application for different languages and regions. The implementation includes the extraction of all of the texts into translation files, the use of libraries such as i18next or react-intl, and the switching of the language through a context or through global state. It matters to take the formatting of the dates, the numbers and the currencies into account.","What is localization?\\nLocalization (i18n for short — from the word internationalization, where 18 is the number of letters between the first and the last) is the process of adapting a software product for use in different countries and cultures. It includes not only the translation of the texts but also the proper display of the dates, the numbers and the currencies, the direction of the text (for Arabic, for example) and other regional particulars.\\n\\nThe main approaches to the implementation\\nIn modern web applications the localization is normally built on the following principles:\\n- All of the user-facing strings are extracted into separate files (JSON, YAML, PO) with keys.\\n- A localization library (i18next, react-intl, vue-i18n, for example) loads the file needed, depending on the language chosen.\\n- The switching of the language happens through global state (Redux, the Context API) or through a URL parameter.\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript with i18next\\n\\nThe practical aspects\\nWhen implementing localization it matters to take the following into account:\\n- Pluralization (the plural forms) — in Russian there are three of them, in English two.\\n- The interpolation of variables inside the strings.\\n- The formatting of the dates and the numbers with the locale in mind (through the Intl API, for example).\\n- The holding of the language chosen in localStorage or in a cookie, in order to preserve the user's preferences.\\n\\nConclusion\\nLocalization is necessary for extending an application's audience to the international market. The use of ready-made libraries and the extraction of the strings into separate files makes the process of translation simple and maintainable and also lets you bring translators in without a knowledge of the code.","// locales/en.json\\n{\\n  \\"greeting\\": \\"Hello, {{name}}!\\"\\n}\\n\\n// locales/fr.json\\n{\\n  \\"greeting\\": \\"Bonjour, {{name}} !\\"\\n}\\n\\n// main.js\\nimport i18next from 'i18next';\\nimport en from './locales/en.json';\\nimport fr from './locales/fr.json';\\n\\ni18next.init({\\n  lng: 'fr',\\n  resources: {\\n    en: { translation: en },\\n    fr: { translation: fr }\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nconsole.log(i18next.t('greeting', { name: 'Anna' })); // Bonjour, Anna !"],[2685,"A role-based access model on the frontend is implemented through the holding of the user's roles and permissions (normally in the token or in a separate request) and the conditional display of the UI elements on the basis of that data. The determination of the rights happens through a check for the presence of a particular role or permission in the component's code. It matters to remember that frontend checks are not security but only improve the UX; the real protection is always on the backend.","The basic concept\\nA role-based access model (RBAC) on the frontend is a way of managing the visibility and the availability of the interface depending on the current user's rights. The essence is that after the authentication the frontend receives information about the user's roles and permissions, saves it in the application's state (in Redux or in React context, for example) and uses it for the conditional rendering of the components, the buttons, the routes and other elements.\\n\\nThe determination of the rights normally happens through a check for the presence of a particular role ('admin', for example) or of a particular permission ('canEditPost', for example) in the user object. For that, helper functions or hooks are created, which encapsulate the checking logic.\\n\\nThe practical implementation\\nLet us look at an example in React. Suppose that after the login we receive a user object with an array of roles and permissions:\\n\\nLet us create a hook for checking the rights:\\n\\nNow in a component the button can be displayed conditionally:\\n\\nFor the routes you can use protected wrappers, which check the role and redirect to the login page or to a 403 if the access is forbidden.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nSuch an approach is used in admin panels, CRM systems, corporate portals and any application in which different categories of user have different levels of access. It improves the user experience by hiding the irrelevant functions, and it makes the navigation easier.\\n\\nAn important warning\\nFrontend checks are not protection against malicious actions. An attacker can get round them by changing the code or the requests. All of the critical operations therefore have to be checked on the server. The frontend only creates the appearance of restrictions for an ordinary user.\\n\\nConclusion\\nA role-based model on the frontend is a convenient tool for adapting the interface to the user's rights. Use it for improving the UX, but always duplicate the checks on the backend, in order to ensure the real security of the data and the functionality.","const user = {\\n  roles: ['admin', 'editor'],\\n  permissions: ['createPost', 'deletePost']\\n};\\n\\nfunction usePermission(permission) {\\n  const { user } = useAuth();\\n  return user?.permissions?.includes(permission) ?? false;\\n}\\n\\nfunction PostActions({ postId }) {\\n  const canDelete = usePermission('deletePost');\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <button>Edit</button>\\n      {canDelete && <button>Delete</button>}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2686,"A hand-written library of components is maintained through single principles for the API and the styles. The theming is implemented by means of CSS variables or a theme context, and the customization through props and slots. It matters to document the components and to version the changes.","The basics of maintaining a library of components\\nMaintaining a hand-written library of components requires strict discipline: a single API, versioning, documentation and testing. Without that the components quickly turn into chaos, where every developer adds their own props and styles. The key principle is encapsulation: a component should manage its own logic and styles but provide flexible points of extension.\\n\\nTheming through CSS variables\\nThe simplest and most powerful way of theming is the use of CSS variables (custom properties). They are inherited and overridden at the level of the container, which lets you change the theme without a rebuild. Define the variables in the root element, for example, and override them for the dark theme.\\n\\nIn JavaScript you can switch the \`data-theme\` attribute on the root element. That works in any framework and requires no additional libraries.\\n\\nCustomization through props and slots\\nThe customization of the components is achieved through props (the variants, the sizes, the states) and slots/children for the insertion of arbitrary content. A button can take a \`variant\` and a \`size\` prop, for example, and can also let you override the icon through children.\\n\\nFor deeper customization you can use render props or a context, but that complicates the API. It is better to observe the principle: 80 % of the cases are covered by props, the rest through CSS variables and slots.\\n\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Version the changes by SemVer, so that the consumers can update safely.\\n- Write documentation with examples for every theme and variant.\\n- Use tests for visual regressions (Storybook + Chromatic, for example).\\n- Avoid hard dependencies on a particular framework, if multi-platform support is planned.\\n\\nConclusion\\nMaintaining a library of components is a balance between flexibility and stability. Theming through CSS variables and customization through props is the standard approach, which is easy to maintain and to extend. Apply it if you need a reusable design system in several projects.",":root {\\n  --color-primary: #3498db;\\n  --spacing-unit: 8px;\\n}\\n\\n[data-theme=\\"dark\\"] {\\n  --color-primary: #2980b9;\\n}\\n\\n.button {\\n  background: var(--color-primary);\\n  padding: calc(var(--spacing-unit) * 2);\\n}\\n\\nfunction Button({ variant = 'primary', size = 'md', children }) {\\n  return <button className={\`button button--\${variant} button--\${size}\`}>{children}</button>;\\n}"],[2687,"Compound Components is a pattern in which a group of components works together as a single whole, sharing state through a context or through cloneElement. It lets you create flexible APIs — \`<Select>\` and \`<Select.Option>\`, for example. When using cloneElement it matters to pass the props carefully, so as not to overwrite the user's own. Context is preferable for the complex cases, since it avoids problems with the nesting.","What are Compound Components?\\nCompound Components is a pattern in React in which several components are united into a single API, working together and sharing internal state. The classic example is \`<Select>\` and \`<Select.Option>\`, where the parent manages the selection while the child elements display the options. This approach improves the readability and the reusability of the code, by letting developers combine the components without an explicit passing of props at every level.\\n\\nThe implementation through Context\\nThe most reliable way is to use React Context. The parent component creates a context and passes the state and the functions through it. The child components obtain access to the context through the \`useContext\` hook. That removes the problems with the nesting and lets you extend the API easily.\\n\\nThe implementation through cloneElement\\nThe alternative way is to use \`React.cloneElement\` for passing props to the child elements. That is simpler but less flexible: if a child element already has a prop with the same name, it will be overwritten. It is also hard to work with deeply nested components.\\n\\nWhat to pay attention to\\n\\n- When using \`cloneElement\`, do not overwrite the user's props unnecessarily — it is better to merge them.\\n- Context suits complex hierarchies better, since it does not depend on the tree's structure.\\n- Always check that the child elements are the components expected, in order to avoid errors.\\n\\nConclusion\\nCompound Components is a powerful pattern for creating flexible, intuitive APIs. Use Context for scalable solutions, and \`cloneElement\` for the simple cases, where several props have to be passed quickly. That is especially useful in libraries of components and in complex forms.","const SelectContext = React.createContext();\\n\\nfunction Select({ children }) {\\n  const [value, setValue] = React.useState('');\\n  return (\\n    <SelectContext.Provider value={{ value, setValue }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </SelectContext.Provider>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Option({ value, children }) {\\n  const { value: selected, setValue } = React.useContext(SelectContext);\\n  return (\\n    <div onClick={() => setValue(value)}>\\n      {children} {selected === value ? '✓' : ''}\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nSelect.Option = Option;\\n\\nfunction Select({ children }) {\\n  const [value, setValue] = React.useState('');\\n  return React.Children.map(children, child =>\\n    React.cloneElement(child, { value, setValue })\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nfunction Option({ value, setValue, selected, children }) {\\n  return <div onClick={() => setValue(value)}>{children}</div>;\\n}"],[2688,"Such a function is normally called pick or pickKeys. It takes an array of keys plus an object and then, by means of reduce, creates a new object including only the properties stated. That is convenient for selecting the data needed out of large objects.","What the pick function does\\nThe \`pick\` function is a widespread utility method, which lets you extract from an object only the properties that are listed in an array of keys. It is useful when only part of the data has to be passed into a component or a function, without copying the whole object.\\n\\nThe implementation through reduce\\nThe \`reduce\` method is ideal for this task, since it lets you accumulate the result in a new object step by step. On every iteration we check whether the current key exists in the original object, and if it does, we add it to the accumulator.\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nSuch a function is often used in Redux for selecting part of the state, in React for passing only the props needed, and in API layers for filtering the incoming data. It helps you avoid superfluous data and makes debugging easier.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse \`pick\` everywhere a subset of an object's properties has to be obtained — that makes the code cleaner and prevents the accidental passing of superfluous fields.","function pick(obj, keys) {\\n  return keys.reduce((acc, key) => {\\n    if (key in obj) {\\n      acc[key] = obj[key];\\n    }\\n    return acc;\\n  }, {});\\n}\\n\\nconst user = { name: 'Alice', age: 30, city: 'NYC' };\\nconst picked = pick(user, ['name', 'city']);\\nconsole.log(picked); // { name: 'Alice', city: 'NYC' }"],[2689,"For the communication between tabs you can use the BroadcastChannel API, which lets you send messages to all of the tabs of the same origin. localStorage with the storage event also suits, which fires in the other tabs when the data changes. Both ways are simple and require no server.","Introduction\\nWhen the user opens several tabs of one site, each of them works in an isolated process. To synchronize the actions — the stopping of the player, for example — you have to use a mechanism of inter-tab communication. The main options: BroadcastChannel, localStorage and postMessage.\\n\\nBroadcastChannel\\nBroadcastChannel creates a channel through which messages can be sent to all of the tabs subscribed to that channel. That is the modern, convenient way.\\n\\nlocalStorage and the storage event\\nIf older browsers have to be supported, you can use localStorage. When a value changes in one tab, the storage event fires in the other tabs.\\n\\npostMessage with window.open\\nIf the tabs were opened through window.open, you can use postMessage for direct communication between the windows. That requires references to the windows to be held, however.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor simple synchronization between tabs, BroadcastChannel suits best — it is clean and leaves no traces in the storage. If support for older browsers is needed, use localStorage. The choice depends on the target audience and on the application's complexity.","// Tab 1: sending a message\\nconst channel = new BroadcastChannel('player');\\nchannel.postMessage({ action: 'stop' });\\n\\n// Tab 2: receiving the message\\nconst channel = new BroadcastChannel('player');\\nchannel.onmessage = (event) => {\\n  if (event.data.action === 'stop') {\\n    player.pause();\\n  }\\n};\\n\\n// Tab 1\\nlocalStorage.setItem('playerState', 'stopped');\\n\\n// Tab 2\\nwindow.addEventListener('storage', (event) => {\\n  if (event.key === 'playerState' && event.newValue === 'stopped') {\\n    player.pause();\\n  }\\n});"],[2690,"The hang happens because the browser's main thread is blocked while the large array of data is processed and rendered. The solution includes breaking the data into parts (chunking), the use of the virtualization of the lists, deferred loading, or Web Workers for the heavy computation.","The cause of the hang\\nWhen a large array of data arrives at the frontend, the browser runs the JavaScript in a single main thread. If that thread is busy processing the data (creating thousands of DOM elements or performing complex computations, for example), it cannot handle the user's events, such as clicks or scrolling. That leads to the interface hanging.\\n\\nHow to solve it\\nThere are several approaches, which can be combined:\\n- Breaking it into parts (chunking) — process the data in portions, using \`setTimeout\` or \`requestAnimationFrame\`, in order to give the browser time to paint the frames.\\n- The virtualization of the lists — render only the visible elements of the list rather than all of them at once. Libraries such as \`react-window\` or \`react-virtualized\` help with that.\\n- Web Workers — perform the heavy computations in a separate thread, so as not to block the main one.\\n- Pagination or lazy loading — request the data in parts as the user scrolls.\\n\\nAn example with chunking\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a combination of virtualization and chunking for large arrays, in order to preserve the responsiveness of the interface. For complex computations, apply Web Workers. That matters especially when working with tables, feeds or charts.","function processLargeArray(data) {\\n  const chunkSize = 100;\\n  let index = 0;\\n\\n  function processChunk() {\\n    const chunk = data.slice(index, index + chunkSize);\\n    // The processing of the chunk — adding it to the DOM, for example\\n    index += chunkSize;\\n    if (index < data.length) {\\n      requestAnimationFrame(processChunk);\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  processChunk();\\n}"],[2691,"A component's render time can be seen by means of the React DevTools, in the Profiler tab. Record a profiling session, perform the actions that cause a render, and you will see every component's duration. You can also use React's Profiler API, by wrapping the component in a \`<Profiler>\` and passing in an onRender callback.","Why measure the render time\\nIn React the rendering is the process of computing the virtual DOM and updating the real DOM. If a component takes too long to render, that can cause delays in the interface. An understanding of where exactly the time is spent helps you optimize the application.\\n\\nThe ways of measuring\\nThe first way is the Profiler built into the React DevTools. Open the Profiler tab, press the record button, perform the actions you need (a click or the typing of text, for example), then stop the recording. You will see a diagram, where every component is shown with its render time. That is the most visual way of analysing it.\\n\\nThe second way is React's programmatic \`Profiler\` API. Wrap the component in a \`<Profiler>\` and pass in an \`onRender\` callback, which receives the information about the render time. That is convenient for the automatic collection of metrics in production.\\n\\nYou can also use \`console.time\` around the render, but that is less precise, since React can batch the updates.\\n\\nThe practical application\\nMeasuring the render time is useful when optimizing complex lists, forms or components with heavy computation. If you see that a component renders too often or for too long, you can apply memoization (\`React.memo\`, \`useMemo\`) or break the component into smaller parts.\\n\\nConclusion: use the React DevTools Profiler for a fast visual analysis, and the \`Profiler\` API for the automatic collection of metrics in tests or in monitoring. That will help you reveal the bottlenecks and make the application more responsive.","import { Profiler } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction onRenderCallback(id, phase, actualDuration, baseDuration, startTime, commitTime) {\\n  console.log(\`The component \${id} rendered in \${actualDuration} ms\`);\\n}\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return (\\n    <Profiler id=\\"MyComponent\\" onRender={onRenderCallback}>\\n      <MyComponent />\\n    </Profiler>\\n  );\\n}"],[2692,"The useRef hook lets you hold a mutable value, which does not cause a re-render of the component when it is updated. It returns an object with a current property, which can be changed directly. That is useful for holding references to DOM elements or any data that should not affect the drawing.","What is useRef?\\nThe \`useRef\` hook in React creates a mutable object, which is preserved between a component's renders. Unlike \`useState\`, a change of the value in a \`useRef\` does not cause a repeat render. That is the key difference: if you have to hold data that does not affect the UI but has to be available at any moment, \`useRef\` is the right choice.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nOn a call of \`useRef(initialValue)\`, an object of the form \`{ current: initialValue }\` is returned. You can change \`current\` freely, and React will not know about it, so the component will not repaint. That is especially useful for:\\n- Holding references to DOM elements (for the focus or for measuring sizes, for example).\\n- Preserving previous values of props or state.\\n- Holding timers or the identifiers of intervals.\\n\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn the example above, a press of the second button increments \`countRef.current\`, but the component does not repaint, so nothing changes on the screen. That demonstrates that \`useRef\` does not affect the render.\\n\\nWhen to use it?\\nUse \`useRef\` when you have to hold data that should not cause an update of the UI. For holding a prop's previous value, in order to compare it in a \`useEffect\`, or for managing the focus on an input, for example. If the data has to be displayed on the screen, use \`useState\`.\\n\\nIn summary: \`useRef\` is a tool for holding mutable values without re-renders. It is indispensable for working with the DOM and for optimizing the performance, when an update of the state is not required.","import React, { useRef, useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const countRef = useRef(0); // we hold the counter without a re-render\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0); // ordinary state\\n\\n  const incrementRef = () => {\\n    countRef.current += 1;\\n    console.log('Ref count:', countRef.current);\\n  };\\n\\n  const incrementState = () => {\\n    setCount(count + 1);\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>State count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={incrementState}>Increment state</button>\\n      <button onClick={incrementRef}>Increment ref (no re-render)</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2693,"In React the useState and useReducer hooks are used for holding local state. useState suits simple values, while useReducer suits complex state logic. Both hooks let a component hold and update data without external libraries.","An introduction to local state\\nLocal state in React is the data that belongs to a particular component and is managed by it directly. It is used for holding temporary values, such as the user's input, visibility flags or the items selected. To work with such state, React provides built-in hooks, which make the management simple and predictable.\\n\\nThe useState hook\\nThe \`useState\` hook is the most widespread way of holding local state. It takes an initial value and returns an array of two elements: the current value and a function for updating it. An update of the state causes a repeat render of the component with the new data.\\n\\nThis example shows how simply a number can be held and changed. \`useState\` is ideal for single values or simple objects, when the update logic is not too complex.\\n\\nThe useReducer hook\\nWhen the state becomes complex — when it contains several fields or requires consecutive updates, for example — it is better to use \`useReducer\`. It works on the principle of Redux's reducer: it takes a reducer function plus the initial state, and returns the current state plus a \`dispatch\` function for sending actions.\\n\\n\`useReducer\` is convenient for managing forms, shopping carts or any state in which the updates depend on the previous values and on the actions.\\n\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- useState — for simple independent values (strings, numbers, booleans).\\n- useReducer — for complex objects, when several actions change the state, or when the update logic is repeated.\\n\\nBoth hooks are part of React's core and require no additional libraries. They cover most of the needs in local state, while for global state other tools are used, such as Context or Redux.\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo hold local state in React, use \`useState\` for the simple cases and \`useReducer\` for more complex logic. These are the basic tools that every developer working with React has to know, since they underlie any interactive interface.","import React, { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The counter: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport React, { useReducer } from 'react';\\n\\nconst initialState = { count: 0 };\\n\\nfunction reducer(state, action) {\\n  switch (action.type) {\\n    case 'increment':\\n      return { count: state.count + 1 };\\n    case 'decrement':\\n      return { count: state.count - 1 };\\n    default:\\n      return state;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The counter: {state.count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'increment' })}>+</button>\\n      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'decrement' })}>-</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2694,"A synthetic event is React's wrapper over a native browser event. It ensures the same behaviour in every browser and is cleaned up automatically once the handling has finished. React passes it into the handlers — onClick, for example. The event has the same properties as the native one, but asynchronous access to it is limited, because of the pooling.","What is a synthetic event?\\nA synthetic event is a cross-browser wrapper, which React creates around a native browser event. When you write \`onClick\` or \`onChange\`, React does not bind the handler to the DOM element directly but delegates it to the root container and passes a synthetic event object into your handler. That is done for uniformity: independently of the browser, the event's properties (\`target\`, \`preventDefault\`, for example) work the same way.\\n\\nWhat is it for?\\nThe main reasons: the unification of the API, the improvement of the performance through delegation, and the automatic clean-up of memory. React uses event pooling: once the handler has been called, the event's properties are nulled, in order to reuse the object. You therefore cannot save an event and use it later — in a \`setTimeout\`, for example; you have to copy the data you need in advance.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nIf you have to use an event asynchronously, save the fields you need in advance: \`const type = event.type;\`.\\n\\nConclusion\\nSynthetic events are React's standard mechanism for handling the user's actions. They make the development easier by hiding the browsers' differences, but they require care in asynchronous work. An understanding of this concept matters for debugging and optimizing React applications.","function Button() {\\n  const handleClick = (event) => {\\n    console.log(event.type); // 'click'\\n    console.log(event.target); // the DOM element\\n    // event is available only synchronously\\n    setTimeout(() => {\\n      console.log(event.type); // an error, or null, because of the pooling\\n    }, 0);\\n  };\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click</button>;\\n}"],[2695,"No, props in React are immutable. A child component cannot change the props passed from the parent directly. Instead you have to use callbacks passed in through props, so that the parent updates the state, or lift the state higher. That ensures a unidirectional data flow and predictability.","The basic concept\\nIn React props are the mechanism for passing data from a parent component to a child one. They are intended for reading only. That is a fundamental principle, which guarantees that the data in the application moves in one direction: from the top down. If a child component could change the props, that would lead to unpredictable changes of the parent's state and would break the integrity of the data.\\n\\nWhy this matters\\nThe immutability of the props lets React determine efficiently when a component has to be repainted. If the props do not change, React can skip the rendering of the child component. Besides, that makes debugging easier: you always know where the data came from and who can change it.\\n\\nHow to update the data properly\\nIf a child component has to change the data, it has to call a function passed in through props from the parent. The parent, for its part, updates its state, and the new props are passed down automatically. That is called \\"lifting state up\\".\\n\\nIn this example the child component does not change the \`count\` prop directly. Instead it calls \`onIncrement\`, which updates the state in the parent.\\n\\nThe exceptions and the nuances\\nSometimes you can meet attempts to change the props — to assign \`props.value = 5\`, for example. That will not work and will cause an error in strict mode. If you need a local mutable value, use \`useState\` inside the child component, initializing it from the prop.\\n\\nConclusion\\nProps in React are immutable by design. To update the data, use callbacks and lifting state up. That makes the data flow predictable and makes maintaining the application easier. If you notice that you are trying to change the props, reconsider the architecture: perhaps the state should live higher up, or in the component itself.","// The parent component\\nfunction Parent() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n\\n  const increment = () => setCount(count + 1);\\n\\n  return <Child count={count} onIncrement={increment} />;\\n}\\n\\n// The child component\\nfunction Child({ count, onIncrement }) {\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>The counter: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={onIncrement}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2696,"The handling of events in React is a way of responding to the user's actions, such as clicks, the typing of text or the submission of forms. Rather than native DOM events, React uses synthetic events, which work the same way in every browser. The handlers are passed as props in the JSX — onClick={handleClick}, for example. It matters to remember that in class components the this context has to be bound, while in functional ones closures are used.","What is the handling of events in React?\\nIn React the handling of events is the mechanism that lets components respond to the user's actions, such as clicks, mouse hovers, the typing of text or the submission of forms. Rather than using native DOM events directly, React creates its own wrapper — the synthetic events. That is done in order to ensure cross-browser compatibility and to improve the performance through the delegation of the events to the root element.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\nSynthetic events have the same interface as the native ones but work the same way in every browser. The handlers are passed as props in the JSX — \`onClick\`, \`onChange\`, \`onSubmit\`, for example. In functional components the handlers are ordinary functions, which receive the event object. In class components you have to be careful with the \`this\` context, since a class's methods are not bound automatically.\\n\\nExamples of the code\\nA functional component with a click handler:\\n\\nA class component with the binding of the context:\\n\\nThe particulars and the nuances\\n\\n- Synthetic events are pooled (reused) for the performance, so you cannot address their properties asynchronously without \`event.persist()\` (in the older versions of React).\\n- In React you cannot return \`false\` from a handler in order to prevent the default behaviour — you have to call \`event.preventDefault()\`.\\n- Event handlers in React are not strings but functions, which makes the code more type-safe and more convenient to test.\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe handling of events in React is the standard way of interacting with the user, which makes work with the DOM easier and ensures predictable behaviour. Use synthetic events for creating interactive interfaces, remember the binding of the context in classes, and remember that the handlers are ordinary functions, which can be reused and tested.","function Button() {\\n  const handleClick = (event) => {\\n    console.log('The button has been pressed', event);\\n  };\\n\\n  return <button onClick={handleClick}>Click me</button>;\\n}\\n\\nclass Button extends React.Component {\\n  constructor(props) {\\n    super(props);\\n    this.handleClick = this.handleClick.bind(this);\\n  }\\n\\n  handleClick(event) {\\n    console.log('The button has been pressed', event);\\n  }\\n\\n  render() {\\n    return <button onClick={this.handleClick}>Click me</button>;\\n  }\\n}"],[2697,"A Subject in RxJS is a special type of Observable, which is at once both observable and an observer. It lets you multicast values to a great many subscribers. The main kinds: the ordinary Subject (it holds no values), BehaviorSubject (it holds the last value), ReplaySubject (it holds a buffer of the last values) and AsyncSubject (it emits only the last value, once the stream has completed).","What is a Subject?\\nA Subject in RxJS is a hybrid of an Observable and an Observer. It lets you not only subscribe to a stream of data but also emit values into it actively. That makes it a convenient tool for organizing the exchange of data between an application's components or modules, especially in reactive architectures.\\n\\nThe main kinds of Subject\\n\\n- Subject — the base type; it holds no previous values. A new subscriber receives only the values that will be emitted after its subscription.\\n- BehaviorSubject — it holds the last value and passes it to a new subscriber at once, on the subscription. It requires an initial value when it is created.\\n- ReplaySubject — it holds a buffer of the last N values (or of the values from a particular interval of time) and passes them to new subscribers.\\n- AsyncSubject — it emits only the last value, but only once the stream has completed. It is useful for operations whose result is known only at the end.\\n\\nAn example of the usage\\n\\nWhere it is applied\\nA Subject is often used for creating services for the exchange of data in Angular (for managing state, for example), for handling the user interface's events, and for implementing an event bus in applications. A BehaviorSubject is convenient for holding the current state (the user's authorization, for example), a ReplaySubject for caching the latest data, and an AsyncSubject for obtaining the result of an asynchronous operation.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of the particular type of Subject depends on the task: if only new values have to be passed, use a Subject; if the current state has to be held, a BehaviorSubject; if a history has to be replayed, a ReplaySubject; and if only the final result matters, an AsyncSubject.","import { Subject, BehaviorSubject, ReplaySubject, AsyncSubject } from 'rxjs';\\n\\n// An ordinary Subject\\nconst subject = new Subject();\\nsubject.subscribe(v => console.log('Subject:', v));\\nsubject.next(1); // the subscriber will receive 1\\n\\n// A BehaviorSubject\\nconst behavior = new BehaviorSubject(0);\\nbehavior.subscribe(v => console.log('Behavior:', v)); // it will receive 0 at once\\nbehavior.next(1); // it will receive 1\\n\\n// A ReplaySubject with a buffer of 2 values\\nconst replay = new ReplaySubject(2);\\nreplay.next(1);\\nreplay.next(2);\\nreplay.subscribe(v => console.log('Replay:', v)); // it will receive 1 and 2\\n\\n// An AsyncSubject\\nconst async = new AsyncSubject();\\nasync.subscribe(v => console.log('Async:', v));\\nasync.next(1);\\nasync.next(2);\\nasync.complete(); // the subscriber will receive only 2"],[2698,"Hydration is the process of attaching event handlers to the HTML rendered on the server. The typical errors arise because of a discrepancy in the markup between the server and the client — because of the use of random values, dates or browser APIs, for example. To avoid them you have to ensure the rendering is deterministic and use effects for the client-side operations.","What hydration is, and why errors arise\\nHydration is the process in which React takes the HTML generated on the server (SSR) and adds interactivity to it: it binds the event handlers, creates the internal state and prepares the component tree. Hydration errors arise when the server's HTML does not match what React expects to render on the client. That leads to warnings in the console and to incorrect behaviour of the interface.\\n\\nThe typical errors and their causes\\n\\n- The use of random values: \`Math.random()\` or \`Date.now()\` in the render, for example. The server and the client obtain different values, which causes a discrepancy.\\n- A dependency on browser APIs: access to \`window\`, \`document\` or \`localStorage\` during the rendering. On the server those objects do not exist, so the code either crashes or returns different data.\\n- Different versions of the libraries: if the server and the client use different versions of React or of other dependencies, the markup can differ.\\n- Ignoring whitespace and line breaks: insignificant differences in the formatting of the HTML can lead to an error.\\n\\nHow to avoid the errors\\nThe main principle is to make the rendering deterministic. All of the data that depends on the time, on randomness or on the environment has to be computed only on the client — inside a \`useEffect\`, for example. You can also use special libraries, such as \`@react-hook/media-query\` or \`use-sync-external-store\`, for working with the browser APIs safely.\\n\\nIt also matters to check that the server and the client rendering use the same data. If you pass the data from the server through \`window.__INITIAL_STATE__\`, make sure it is serialized the same way.\\n\\nConclusion\\nHydration is a powerful tool for improving the performance and the SEO, but it requires care. Avoid non-deterministic values in the render, use effects for the client-side operations and watch the consistency of the data. That will let you avoid most of the errors and will ensure the application works stably.","// Bad: a random value in the render\\nfunction Component() {\\n  return <div>{Math.random()}</div>;\\n}\\n\\n// Good: the value is computed after the hydration\\nfunction Component() {\\n  const [value, setValue] = React.useState(null);\\n  React.useEffect(() => {\\n    setValue(Math.random());\\n  }, []);\\n  return <div>{value}</div>;\\n}"],[2699,"Stateful components hold and manage their own state through useState or through classes, which lets them change the data and respond to events. Stateless components have no internal state and depend entirely on their props, which makes them predictable and easy to test. In modern React functional components are preferred, while the state is moved into hooks or into global stores.","The basic concept\\nIn React components are divided into two types: stateful (with state) and stateless (without state). Stateful components can hold and change data inside themselves, using mechanisms such as useState or class methods. Stateless components are pure functions, which receive data through props and simply display it, having no state of their own.\\n\\nExamples of the code\\nLet us look at a simple example. A stateless component displays a greeting:\\n\\nA stateful component can change its state — a counter, for example:\\n\\nThe practical application\\nStateless components are useful for displaying static data, such as lists, cards or headings. They are simpler to understand and to reuse. Stateful components are applied for interactive elements: forms, modal windows, timers. In large applications the state is often moved into global stores (Redux, Context), in order to reduce the number of stateful components and make the architecture more predictable.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse stateless components wherever possible, in order to make the code and the testing simpler. Stateful components are justified when local state really is necessary, but try to minimize their number by moving the complex logic into hooks or external stores.","function Greeting({ name }) {\\n  return <h1>Hello, {name}!</h1>;\\n}\\n\\nimport { useState } from 'react';\\n\\nfunction Counter() {\\n  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);\\n  return (\\n    <div>\\n      <p>Count: {count}</p>\\n      <button onClick={() => setCount(count + 1)}>Increment</button>\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[2700,"The holding of heterogeneous data in a single global Redux store is a widespread practice, but it requires careful structuring. Redux assumes one store for the whole state, which makes debugging and synchronization easier; as the application grows, however, it can lead to excessive coupling and complexity. It is recommended that you separate the data into slices (reducers) and use selectors for isolating the logic.","The concept of a single store in Redux\\nRedux, by its philosophy, uses a single global store, which holds the whole of an application's state. That lets you have a single source of truth, makes debugging easier (with the Redux DevTools, for example) and makes the state predictable. The holding of heterogeneous data, such as the user's profile and the cart, in one store is not a mistake, however — that is the standard approach. The problems arise when the store's structure is not organized properly.\\n\\nThe effect on the architecture\\nIf all of the data lies in one store without a clear separation, that can lead to the following problems: the difficulty of maintenance, difficulties with scaling, redundant repaints of the components, and coupling between unrelated modules. To avoid that, Redux offers you the ability to break the state into slices, by means of the combination of the reducers. Every slice is responsible for its own domain area — \`user\` and \`cart\`, for example. That preserves the single store while making it structured.\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nSuch an approach lets every module work only with its own part of the store, while the selectors help isolate the data. The cart component uses \`state.cart\`, for example, and the profile \`state.user\`.\\n\\nWhen this is justified\\nA single store is justified for medium and large applications, where the centralized management of the state matters. For very large projects you can consider alternatives, such as modular state (Zustand, for example), but Redux remains a reliable choice, thanks to its predictability and its tools.\\n\\nIn summary: the holding of heterogeneous data in one Redux store is a good practice, if the state is structured properly through slices and selectors. That makes the architecture simpler and scalable and makes debugging easier, but it requires discipline in the organization of the code.","// store.js\\nimport { combineReducers, createStore } from 'redux';\\nimport userReducer from './userReducer';\\nimport cartReducer from './cartReducer';\\n\\nconst rootReducer = combineReducers({\\n  user: userReducer,\\n  cart: cartReducer\\n});\\n\\nconst store = createStore(rootReducer);\\n// Now the state is: { user: {...}, cart: {...} }"]]`);export{e as default};